\title{Efficient Estimation of Logical Sensitivities Through Fault-Counting}
\author{Winston Fu\textsuperscript{1}}
\email{winston.fu@princeton.edu}
\author{J. Wilson Staples\textsuperscript{1}}
\author{Jeff D. Thompson\textsuperscript{1,2}}
\affiliation{\textsuperscript{1}Princeton Quantum Initiative, Princeton University, Princeton, NJ 08544, USA}
\affiliation{\textsuperscript{2}Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering, Princeton University, Princeton, NJ 08544, USA}

\noindent

\date{\today}

\begin{abstract}

Quantum error-correcting circuits are affected by multiple physical noise mechanisms, whose contributions to logical failure must be understood to evaluate code performance and guide improvements in hardware.
The individual error budget contributions for an error type $i$ can be characterized by its logical sensitivity $\nu_i = \pdv*{p_L}{p_i}$, which measures the response of the logical error rate $p_L$ to each physical noise parameter $p_i$. Normally, $\nu_i$ is measured using linear fits such as finite differences, where $p_L$ is measured at two or more values of $p_i$ to calculate partial derivatives. In this paper, we develop a differentiable estimator to obtain all components of $\nabla_{\mathbf p}p_L$ simultaneously from a single Monte Carlo data set at one noise configuration, using information about the underlying fault configurations.
In surface code simulations with circuit-level noise, the estimator agrees with conventional finite differences while requiring one to two orders of magnitude fewer shots to achieve the same variance. We apply this technique to quantify error budgets, resolve sensitivities at the individual qubit level, and infer effective code distance. Finally, we incorporate the sensitivities into Newton root finding to locate and trace threshold contours in multidimensional noise models. These results provide an efficient method for extracting and applying logical sensitivity information from standard quantum error correction simulations.

\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}
Quantum error correction (QEC) suppresses physical noise by encoding information redundantly and non-locally. 
Physical qubits are subject to multiple noise mechanisms, and understanding how each component contributes to logical failure is important for accurately estimating code performance \cite{iyerSmallQuantumComputer2017,chenExponentialSuppressionBit2021,acharyaSuppressingQuantumErrors2023,acharyaQuantumErrorCorrection2025,deMartiiOliusPerformanceSurfaceCodes2022,puttermanHardwareefficientQuantumError2025a}. Real devices contain distinct error mechanisms associated with gates, idling, state preparation, and measurement, whose relative importance can differ substantially.

A natural local characterization of this dependence is the logical sensitivity, $\nu_i(\pvec)\equiv \pdv*{p_L(\pvec)}{p_i}$, where $\pvec=(p_1,\ldots,p_s)$
is the vector of physical noise parameters and $p_i$ is the physical error rate associated with noise channel $i$. Reducing the error rate of an error type with higher logical sensitivity would cause a larger reduction in logical error rate. The dimension $s$ of $\pvec$ depends on the features of the error model that are the subject of study, and can range from simply grouping errors into types (single-qubit, two-qubit, idle, etc.) to parametrizing individual space-time locations~\cite{chadwickErasureMinesweeperExploring2025,carrollSubsystemSurfaceCompass2024,tiurevCorrectingNonindependentNonidentically2023}.

The standard numerical approach estimates each $\nu_i$ by finite differences \cite{chenExponentialSuppressionBit2021,acharyaSuppressingQuantumErrors2023,acharyaQuantumErrorCorrection2025,eickbuschDemonstrationDynamicSurface2025a,puttermanHardwareefficientQuantumError2025a}: perturb $p_i$, rerun the circuit simulation and decoder, and divide the resulting change in $p_L$ by the perturbation size. This incurs a separate set of simulations for every parameter, so its cost grows linearly with $s$. The step size must be small enough to control Taylor series truncation error, but large enough for the change in $p_L$ to be statistically resolved. These limitations lead to a high computational cost when characterizing circuit-level noise models with fine-grained parameterization of $\pvec$.

In experiments on quantum devices, we have access to the measured syndromes and the logical outcome of the decoder. However, in stabilizer tableau simulations, we also have access to the underlying fault configurations that produced these syndromes. While usually treated as internal bookkeeping, these fault configurations contain additional information that can be used to efficiently estimate quantities other than the logical error rate.

In this paper, we develop a differentiable estimator that obtains all components of $\nabla_{\pvec}p_L$ from a single Monte Carlo data set (Fig.~\ref{fig:overview}). It is a novel application of the score-function estimator \cite{rubinsteinScoreFunctionApproach1986,glynnLikelihoodRatioGradient1990}, which has previously been used in QEC for reinforcement-learning-based optimization of decoder priors \cite{sivakOptimizationDecoderPriors2024}, control parameters \cite{sivakRealtimeQuantumError2023,sivakReinforcementLearningControl2026}, and code constructions \cite{olleSimultaneousDiscoveryQuantum2024,suDiscoveryOptimalQuantum2025}, where the score function is taken with respect to a distribution used to sample candidate parameter choices. Instead, here we apply the score function directly to the distribution of physical fault configurations, yielding derivatives of the logical error rate with respect to the underlying physical noise parameters from the same samples used to estimate $p_L$.

We validate the estimator in circuit-level surface code simulations by comparing it directly with finite differences across physical error rates and code distances. We then test this method as a subroutine in an algorithm which maps threshold surfaces in multi-parameter noise models \cite{dawsonNoiseThresholdsOptical2006,svoreFlowmapModelAnalyzing2006,staceThresholdsTopologicalCodes2009,bonillaAtaidesXZZXSurfaceCode2021,vodolaFundamentalThresholds2022}. These results establish logical sensitivity as an easily accessible diagnostic for QEC design and hardware optimization that can be estimated from the same simulation samples used to estimate logical error rates.

