\section{Efficient Sensitivity Estimation}
\subsection{Algorithm}

Consider a stochastic error model consisting of a set $\Omega$ of independently sampled error mechanisms. The model is governed by a set of physical error parameters $\vb p$. The parametrization may use a single shared error rate, as is the case in a code-capacity noise model, or every spacetime fault location $f \in \Phi$ may be assigned a separate parameter $p_f$. The commonly used circuit-level noise model is an intermediate example, where faults of the same error type use a shared parameter. Each error mechanism $e\in\Omega$ is sampled with probability $W_e(\pvec)$. For a given shot, let $E\subseteq\Omega$ denote the set of mechanisms that occur, and let the decoder outcome be represented by the indicator function
\begin{equation}
        L(E)
    =
    \mathds{1}\{E \text{ causes a logical error}\},
    \qquad
    E\subseteq\Omega.
\end{equation}
The logical error rate can be written as
\begin{equation}
    \pl(\pvec)
    =
    \sum_{E \subseteq \Omega}
    L(E)\,\pr_{\pvec}(E) = \expec_{E \sim \pr_{\vb p}}[L(E)],
\end{equation}
where, under the assumption that faults are sampled independently,
\begin{equation}
    \pr_{\pvec}(E)
    =
    \prod_{e \in E} W_e(\pvec)
    \prod_{e \notin E} \bigl(1-W_e(\pvec)\bigr).
\end{equation}

The logical sensitivity can then be expressed as:
\begin{align}
    \nu_i(\pvec)
    &\equiv
    \pdv{\pl(\pvec)}{p_i}
    \notag\\
    &=
    \sum_{E\subseteq\Omega}
    L(E)\,\pr_{\pvec}(E)
    \pdv{p_i}\log \pr_{\pvec}(E)
    \notag\\
    &=
    \expec_{E\sim\pr_{\pvec}}
    \left[
        L(E)
        \pdv{p_i}\log \pr_{\pvec}(E)
    \right]
    \notag\\
    &=
    \expec_{E\sim\pr_{\pvec}}
    \Biggl[
        L(E)\Biggl(
            \sum_{e\in E}
            \frac{1}{W_e(\pvec)}
            \pdv{W_e(\pvec)}{p_i}
    \notag\\
    &\qquad\qquad {}
            -
            \sum_{e\notin E}
            \frac{1}{1-W_e(\pvec)}
            \pdv{W_e(\pvec)}{p_i}
        \Biggr)
    \Biggr].
    \label{eq:sens_model}
\end{align}
Intuitively, the sensitivity measures how strongly faults of a given type are represented in failing fault configurations. Every sensitivity $\nu_i(\vb p)$ can be estimated from the same set of data that was used to estimate $p_L(\vb p)$. Additionally, under the fault-level parametrization, the fact that all $\nu_f$ can be found at once means that the grouping of faults does not have to be decided beforehand. Instead, the sensitivity for any grouping of faults $\nu_{F}$ can be found by storing and summing the individual sensitivity components $\nu_f$. While second- and higher-order derivatives can also be calculated using this method, we will consider only first-order derivatives in this paper for simplicity.

\subsection{Numerics}

We benchmarked the differentiable method by running simulations of the surface code with circuit-level noise in Stim \cite{gidneyStimFastStabilizer2021}. Details of how the sampler calculates $W_e(\vb p)$ given $\vb p$ are given in Appendix~\ref{app:inc_err}.

We compared the differentiable estimator (\diff{}) with the 
finite-difference estimator (\fd{}), corresponding to the simplest local straight-line fit. The differentiable estimator given $N$ shots from Eq.~\ref{eq:sens_model} is
\begin{equation}
    \hat{\nu}_i^{\mathrm{Diff}}
    =
    \frac{1}{N}
    \sum_{k=1}^{N}
    L(E_k)
    \pdv{p_i}
    \log \pr_{\pvec}(E_k).
\end{equation}

The finite-difference estimator is:
\begin{equation}
    \hat{\nu}_i^{\mathrm{FD}}
    =
    \frac{
        \hat p_{L, d}(\pvec + h \mathbf e_i)
        -
        \hat p_{L, d}(\pvec - h \mathbf e_i)
    }{2h},
\end{equation}
where $\hat p_{L,d}(\vb p) = \frac{1}{N} \sum_{k=1}^{N} L(E_k)$ with error mechanisms $E_k$ sampled from the distribution corresponding to the distance-$d$ circuit parametrized by $\pvec$. $h$ is the finite-difference step size, and $\mathbf e_i$ denotes the unit vector in the
$p_i$ direction. The decoder is held fixed at $\vb p$.

\begin{figure*}
    \centering

    \caption{(a) Logarithmic sensitivities $\pdv*{\log p_L}{\log p_i}$, over a range of $p$. Circles are the estimates obtained using \diff{}, while diamonds are estimates obtained using \fd{}. (b) Relative difference between $\hat \nu_{2\text{qb}}^\text{FD}$ and $\hat \nu_{2\text{qb}}^\text{Diff}$ at different step fractions $a$, for $d=7$ at $p=0.005$. As the sensitivity is measured over a larger range of $p$, higher-order terms cause a bias in the measured sensitivity. (c) Fractional variance in sensitivity from \diff{} and \fd{} at varying shot budgets, obtained from 200 bootstrap samples of $2\times 10^9$ shots for each error type at step fraction $a=0.1$.}
    \label{fig:diff_vs_fd}
\end{figure*}

We first tested the accuracy of \diff{} over a range of $10^{-3} \leq p \leq 10^{-2}$, $p_i = p$ for all $i$, under circuit-level noise for the unrotated surface code for distances 3, 5, and 7. \diff{} was used to estimate the individual sensitivities $\nu_i$ at 6 points with $10^9$ shots each. These were compared at three $p$ values with \fd{} with step fraction $a = h/p_i = 0.1$. For \fd{}, we ran two simulations per error type, with $10^9$ shots
per simulation except at $p=10^{-3}$, where we used $3\times10^9$. For a clearer comparison across error scales, we compared the logarithmic sensitivity, $\pdv*{\log p_L}{\log p_i} = \nu_i p_i/p_L$, in Fig.~\ref{fig:diff_vs_fd}a. The small values of $\nu_i$ for single-qubit gate and measurement errors made it difficult to measure precisely without introducing bias with \fd{}, leading to large error bars at $p=10^{-3}$. The two estimators displayed close numerical agreement across the configurations tested.

The step fraction was chosen to limit finite-step bias in \fd{}. For example, Fig.~\ref{fig:diff_vs_fd}b shows the difference between $\hat \nu_{2\text{qb}}^\text{FD}$ and $\hat \nu_{2\text{qb}}^\text{Diff}$ as $a$ was varied at $d=7$, $p=0.005$. A measurable bias was observed at $a=0.2$, and continued increasing with larger step sizes.

Next, we compared the performance of the two estimators when calculating $\nu_i$ for 5 error types under equivalent shot budgets (Fig.~\ref{fig:diff_vs_fd}c). Using $a=0.1$, we measured the variance when using a fixed number of shots to measure $\nu$ for 5 error types at $p=0.005$. This was done over a range of shot budgets between 100 and $2\times 10^9$, where the data for smaller shot budgets were obtained through 200 bootstrap resamples of the data set collected at the largest shot budget. The variance ratios between the two estimators remained approximately constant across the different shot budgets. The values agree with the predicted ratio that we expect from treating logical failures as Bernoulli random events (Appendix~\ref{app:uncertainty}):
\begin{equation}
    R_i = \frac{\variance(\hat{\nu}_i^{\mathrm{FD}})}{\variance(\hat{\nu}_i^{\mathrm{Diff}})}
    \approx
    \frac{s}{a^2 \mathbb E[M_i^2 \mid L]},
    \label{eq:variance_ratio}
\end{equation}
where $\mathbb E[M_i^2 \mid L]$ is the expected squared number of channel-$i$ faults conditioned on logical errors.  The factor of $s$ in Eq.~\ref{eq:variance_ratio} accounts for the splitting of shot budget using each estimator. Using \diff{}, one could use the entire shot budget on a single experiment at $p=0.005$. However, using \fd{}, one needed to split the shot budget over 10 separate experiments: two for each of the 5 error types at $p_i = (1 \pm 0.1)p$. Furthermore, sampling noise is amplified by the size of the finite step in \fd{}. Hence, for the configurations tested, \diff{} reduces the number of shots required to estimate $\nu_i$ by one to two orders of magnitude over \fd{}.

