    \label{fig:diff_resolution}
\end{figure*}

\diff{} allows for the measurement of sensitivities in a circuit at a per-fault level, using the same simulation data used to estimate $p_L$ with additional processing of the sampled fault configurations. For our noise model, this corresponds to $s=268,\ 1526$, and 4544 for distances 3, 5, and 7 respectively. $\mathbb E[M_i^2 \mid L]$ in Eq.~\ref{eq:variance_ratio} scales at least as $1/s$ when subdividing a fixed number of faults, hence $R_i$ scales with the square of the number of error types defined for the circuit. Fig.~\ref{fig:diff_resolution}a shows the varying levels of resolution possible when estimating sensitivities with \diff{}. Firstly, along every point of a $p_L$ vs $p$ curve, the sensitivities can be aggregated by error type. Such decompositions help identify which physical processes to prioritize for improvement, as illustrated by experimental error budgets separating gate, measurement, idling, and qubit loss contributions~\cite{acharyaQuantumErrorCorrection2025,eickbuschDemonstrationDynamicSurface2025a,puttermanHardwareefficientQuantumError2025a}. These values at different distances can also be used to calculate logical error scaling budget components:
\begin{equation}
    \hat{\pdv{\Lambda_d^{-1}}{p_i}} =  \frac{\hat p_{L,d+2}}{\hat p_{L,d}} \left(
    \frac{\hat{\nu}^{\mathrm{Diff}}_{d+2,i}}{\hat p_{L,d+2}}
    - \frac{\hat{\nu}^{\mathrm{Diff}}_{d,i}}{\hat p_{L,d}}
  \right),
  \label{eq:lambda_comp}
\end{equation}
where $\Lambda_d = p_{L, d} / p_{L, d+2}$.

However, recent studies in hybrid-erasure architectures \cite{chadwickErasureMinesweeperExploring2025} and heterogeneous noise models \cite{deMartiiOliusPerformanceSurfaceCodes2022,Khosravani:2026jma} have revealed a strong spatial dependence of the logical performance of error-correcting codes on individual qubit characteristics. Motivated by these examples, we demonstrate applying \diff{} to resolve the position-dependent sensitivity to each error type. For instance, the plotted spatial heatmaps show a higher relative importance of qubits in the bulk compared to the boundary, with sensitivities decreasing from the center of the code patch. These heatmaps can help inform decisions about resource allocation for hardware improvements. Thus, a single simulation campaign supports both coarse hardware error budgets and fine-grained circuit diagnostics, with the grouping chosen after sampling rather than fixed in advance. 

\diff{} can also be used to find the local effective distance scaling factor at every point. Along the uniform-noise path $p_i=p$ for all $i$, define $\hat\nu^{\mathrm{Diff}} = \sum_i \hat\nu^{\mathrm{Diff}}_i$. This estimates the total derivative $\dv*{p_L}{p}$. Starting from the ansatz $\pl = A\left( {p}/{p_\text{th}} \right)^{(d+1)/2 }$, we differentiate to obtain the distance estimator $\hat{d} = 2\left( \hat\nu^{\mathrm{Diff}} {p}/{\hat p_L} \right) - 1$. This is shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:diff_resolution}b, and provides a useful diagnostic of fault-tolerance properties of a circuit.

