\section{Efficient Threshold Estimation}

\subsection{Algorithm}

Finally, we applied our efficient sensitivity estimator to the problem of finding finite-distance code thresholds. While the threshold error rate of a code is often taken to be a single scalar value, a more accurate picture is to consider a surface in the multidimensional error space.
Such threshold surfaces have been studied for a variety of codes and noise models, including concatenated codes with multiple circuit-level error rates \cite{svoreFlowmapModelAnalyzing2006,dawsonNoiseThresholdsOptical2006}, surface codes subject to qubit loss \cite{staceThresholdsTopologicalCodes2009}, and surface codes under biased Pauli noise \cite{bonillaAtaidesXZZXSurfaceCode2021}.
The usual method for finding the threshold of a QEC code involves sampling at a range of error rates close to the threshold, then fitting the data to a model \cite{svoreFlowmapModelAnalyzing2006,staceThresholdsTopologicalCodes2009,bonillaAtaidesXZZXSurfaceCode2021,brownFaulttolerantErrorCorrection2016}. Doing so is computationally expensive, as it requires sampling over a wide range of error parameters. This problem is made worse when finding the threshold surface for multiple error types. If $\mathcal{O}(K)$ points were needed to find the threshold for an error rate parametrized by a scalar, then $s$ error types would require repeating this process many times. Up to $\mathcal{O}(K^s)$ points need to be sampled to fully cover the threshold surface. This has motivated the use of approximate threshold-finding algorithms, such as detecting crossings in logical error rate from samples at varying distances and error rates \cite{zenLowvalencyScalableQuantum2026}.
Furthermore, it has been shown that the critical exponent model used for threshold fitting does not account for all finite-size corrections \cite{xiaoExactResultsFinite2024}, making it desirable to estimate the threshold in a model-agnostic manner.

The algorithm we propose employs sensitivity information in Newton--Raphson root finding to locate the crossing in logical error rate between two code distances. Define the crossing function:
\begin{equation}
    \Delta(p) \equiv \Delta_{d_1, d_2}(p) = \log p_L(d_2, p) - \log p_L(d_1, p).
\end{equation}
The root of this equation, $\Delta(p_\text{th}) = 0$, gives the finite-distance threshold with respect to the two distances $d_1$ and $d_2$.
Then, we can iteratively find the root with the standard procedure:
\begin{equation}
    p^{(1)} = p^{(0)} - \frac{\Delta(p^{(0)})}{\Delta'(p^{(0)})},
\end{equation}
where
\begin{equation}
    \Delta'(p) = \frac{1}{p_L(d_2, p)}  \nu(d_2, p) - \frac{1}{p_L(d_1, p)} \nu(d_1, p).
\end{equation}

In the $s$-dimensional case, $\Delta$ is a function of the full physical-noise vector,
\begin{equation}
    \Delta(\pvec)
    =
    \log p_L(d_2,\pvec)-\log p_L(d_1,\pvec),
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
    \pvec^{(1)}
    =
    \pvec^{(0)}
    -
    \frac{\nabla_{\pvec}\Delta(\pvec^{(0)})}
    {\left\|\nabla_{\pvec}\Delta(\pvec^{(0)})\right\|^2}
    \Delta(\pvec^{(0)}),
\end{equation}
where $\pvec^{(0)}$ and $\pvec^{(1)}$ are successive points in the $s$-dimensional physical-noise space.

By using $\hat\nu^{\mathrm{Diff}}$ as the estimator for $\nu$, a pair of experiments, one at each distance, is sufficient to measure both $\Delta(\pvec)$ and $\nabla_{\pvec}\Delta(\pvec)$ simultaneously.

After finding one point on the threshold hypersurface, we also learn its normal vector, giving us $(s-1)$ linearly independent tangent directions in probability space where $\Delta$ remains constant to first order. In the $s=2$ case, this corresponds to a local tangent to the threshold contour line. Note that as we are working at finite distances, the resulting crossing contours only approximate the asymptotic threshold.

For the two-dimensional slices considered below, the complete contour-tracing procedure is illustrated in Fig.~\ref{fig:thres_finder_explanation}a and detailed in Algorithm~\ref{alg:threshold_contour}.

\begin{figure*}
    \centering

    \caption{(a) Illustration of the threshold-finding algorithm for the case $s=2$. The gray line shows the threshold contour between error types $p_1$ and $p_2$ that needs to be found. Boxed numbers represent the steps of the algorithm. Step 1: For two distances $d_1$ and $d_2$, sample the circuits at some $\vb p_0$, measure $p_L$, and calculate $\Delta(\vb p_0)$, while also using \diff{} to find $\grad_{\vb p} \Delta(\vb p_0)$ from the same set of data. Step 2: The Newton step predicts a new point $\vb p_1$ for which $\Delta(\vb p_1) = 0$. Repeat until the convergence criteria are met. Step 3: Choosing a $\vb p_2$ where $\vb p_2 - \vb p_1$ is orthogonal to the vector $\grad_{\vb p} \Delta(\vb p_1)$. This leaves $\Delta(\vb p_2) = 0$ to first order, and so is close to the threshold as well. Repeat root finding until this next point converges, and continue until the desired range of the threshold contour is obtained. (b) Data from a simulation of the surface code at distances 5 and 7, decoded with PyMatching, that shows all the points that needed to be sampled to trace the threshold contour between two-qubit gate and idle errors. Red square: initial point. Red crosses: points that did not fit the convergence criteria, and required further Newton steps. Black circles: converged points that lie on the crossing between $d=5$ and 7 codes.}
    \label{fig:thres_finder_explanation}
\end{figure*}

\begin{algorithm}[H]
\caption{Sensitivity-guided threshold contour tracing}
\label{alg:threshold_contour}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Require Initial guess $\pvec_0$, distances $d_1,d_2$, step size $h$,
         fractional tolerance $\epsilon$, number of steps $r$
\Ensure List of contour points $\mathcal{C}$

\Function{FindThres}{$\pvec$}
    \State $\mathcal D \gets \varnothing$
    \While{true}
        \State Sample shots at $\pvec$ for both distances;
        \State Append new samples to $\mathcal D$

        \State Estimate $\Delta$, $\sigma_\Delta$, and
               $\mathbf n=\nabla_{\pvec}\Delta$ from $\mathcal D$
        \If{$|\Delta|>2\sigma_\Delta$}
            \State $\displaystyle
                \pvec\gets\pvec
                -\frac{\Delta}{\|\mathbf n\|_2^2}\mathbf n$
            \State $\mathcal D\gets\varnothing$
        \ElsIf{$\displaystyle
            \frac{\sigma_\Delta}{|\pvec\cdot\mathbf n|}
            \le\epsilon$}
            \State \Return $(\pvec,\mathbf n)$
        \EndIf
    \EndWhile
\EndFunction

\State $(\pvec,\mathbf n)\gets\Call{FindThres}{\pvec_0}$
\State $\mathcal{C}\gets[\pvec]$

\For{$k=1,\ldots,r$}
    \State Choose $\mathbf t$ such that
           $\mathbf n\cdot\mathbf t=0$ and $\|\mathbf t\|_2=1$
           \Comment{tangent at the current anchor}
    \State $\pvec_{\mathrm{pred}}\gets\pvec+h\mathbf t$
    \State $(\pvec,\mathbf n)
           \gets\Call{FindThres}{\pvec_{\mathrm{pred}}}$
           \Comment{update the anchor and normal}
    \State Append $\pvec$ to $\mathcal{C}$
\EndFor

\State \Return $\mathcal{C}$
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\subsection{Numerics}
We demonstrated the effectiveness of using logical sensitivities to guide the tracing of threshold contours by running simulations on the surface code with a variety of decoders.
We ran Alg.~\ref{alg:threshold_contour} to trace the contours for the four combinations of error types, while holding the remaining error rates fixed.
We used an iterative refinement approach. First, $2\times 10^6$ shots were taken at one point. If $|\Delta| > 2 \sigma_\Delta$, then the noise parameters were updated according to Newton root finding. If $|\Delta| \leq 2 \sigma_\Delta$, then we checked whether $\sigma_\Delta/ {|\pvec\cdot\mathbf n|} \le\epsilon$, with more shots taken at that same point until this cutoff was met. This corresponds to the uncertainty in the normal direction to the contour, normalized by the perpendicular distance from the tangent to the origin. As $\sigma_\Delta$ was decreased, the residual could become statistically significant again, requiring another Newton step. Otherwise, a point was considered converged once both criteria were met.
This was done using distances $(3, 5)$ and $(5, 7)$ with PyMatching \cite{higgottSparseBlossomCorrecting2025a} and Correlated PyMatching, and $(3, 5)$ with Tesseract \cite{beniTesseractSearchBasedDecoder2025b}. Fig.~\ref{fig:thres_finder_explanation}b shows a representative run for distances (5,7) with PyMatching. All the measured contours are shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:contour}, with independent samples at each converged point verifying that all points fall within $2 \sigma$ of $\Delta=0$.

Using the sensitivity at previous points to predict subsequent points on the threshold contour reduces the number of points that must be sampled to find a crossing between codes of different distances. Fig.~\ref{fig:thres_finder_explanation}b shows the points that were sampled to trace the threshold contour between two-qubit gate and idle errors. The predicted locations
for the points where $\Delta=0$, based on local sensitivity information, lay close to the actual threshold, and required only small refinements to converge. By avoiding repeated scans across the crossing contour, the number of points needed using Newton root finding is $\mathcal{O}(K^{s-1})$, representing a factor $K$ improvement over grid sampling.

