    \centering

    \caption{Threshold contours for four error type pairs, using PyMatching, Correlated PyMatching, and Tesseract. Uncertainty in contour position due to statistical uncertainty in $\Delta$ is plotted as a lightly shaded region around each contour line. For PyMatching and Correlated PyMatching, darker shades show the thresholds found using the $(5, 7)$ distance pair. Lighter shades show the $(3, 5)$ distance pair. For Tesseract, only the $(3, 5)$ distance pair was measured.}
    \label{fig:contour}
\end{figure*}

\section{Discussion}

We have presented a differentiable method that estimates logical sensitivities to all physical noise parameters from a single Monte Carlo data set. In circuit-level surface code simulations, the estimator agrees with finite differences and achieves lower variance at a fixed total shot budget. \diff{} makes use of sampled fault configurations, in combination with decoder logical outcomes, to estimate sensitivities at the individual fault level. This enables arbitrary post-hoc aggregation of sensitivities and error budgets at different levels of resolution, from individual qubits to error types.

Incorporating \diff{} into Newton root finding enables efficient threshold contour estimation in multi-parameter noise models, using model-agnostic explicit distance crossings. Together, these results provide a practical tool for diagnosing QEC circuits and identifying which physical errors most strongly affect logical performance.

For future work, the differentiable estimator could be extended to second- and higher-order derivatives. These derivatives account for the curvature of the threshold contour, allowing more accurate predictions of the threshold. In addition, this information may be applied to rare-event sampling to increase the probability of sampling harmful correlated fault configurations. Although our numerical demonstrations used Pauli noise, \diff{} applies equally to any independently sampled stochastic event, such as leakage, erasure \cite{wuErasureConversionFaulttolerant2022a,chadwickErasureMinesweeperExploring2025}, high-weight correlated errors \cite{mcewenResolvingCatastrophicError2022}, and non-Clifford errors \cite{harperNonCliffordCrosstalkNoise2026,cercelescuPolynomialtimeSimulationNonClifford2026}. These mechanisms enable more realistic modelling of quantum hardware, but can be costly to simulate. \diff{} could be used to efficiently extract the sensitivities for these noise mechanisms from simulations to better guide hardware improvements.

