\affiliation{School of Electrical and Computer Engineering, Cornell University, Ithaca, New
York 14850, USA}
\begin{abstract}
    In quantum reading, classical messages are encoded in sequences of quantum channels and recovered by probing the channels and processing their outputs. We study the reading capacity of a finite family of finite-dimensional channels under an average constraint on the probing energy, allowing arbitrary adaptive operations between channel uses. Using an input-dependent chain rule for the Belavkin–Staszewski relative entropy, we derive a converse bound expressed as an optimization involving channel Choi operators and the operator relative entropy. This formulation admits semidefinite approximations and reduces, without an energy constraint, to a channel information radius. To obtain achievable rates, we construct bilinear semidefinite lower approximations to a standard non-adaptive reading bound. Alternating optimization produces feasible probe states and encoding distributions, whose Holevo information gives a directly evaluable achievable rate. For jointly classical–quantum channels, we derive a separate converse based on the Umegaki relative entropy. Without an energy constraint, this converse matches a non-adaptive achievable rate, recovering a recent result of Pascual Abraldes and Winter: non-adaptive protocols suffice to achieve the unconstrained reading capacity of jointly classical--quantum channels.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{Background and motivation}

Quantum information theory has identified information processing tasks in which quantum resources improve performance beyond that attainable with classical strategies. Quantum metrology~\cite{GLM11} and quantum illumination~\cite{Lloyd08} are prominent examples. Quantum reading provides another setting in which the choice of a quantum probe can have an operational advantage~\cite{Pirandola11,PLGMB11}. In this task, an encoder stores a classical message in a sequence of memory cells, each of which implements a quantum channel selected from a prescribed family. A reader retrieves the message by preparing probe systems, allowing them to interact with the memory cells, and processing the resulting outputs. The relevant resources therefore include the probe states, any retained ancillary systems, and the operations used to decode the message.

The original optical formulation of quantum reading demonstrated that nonclassical light can outperform classical mixtures of coherent states when distinguishing memory cells with different reflectivities under a constraint on the mean number of incident photons~\cite{Pirandola11}. Ref.~\cite{PLGMB11} subsequently developed a capacity formulation based on encoding across multiple cells and collective decoding. The quantum reading capacity quantifies the largest asymptotic rate, in bits per cell, at which classical messages can be recovered with vanishing error. This formulation connects the task of channel discrimination with the coding problem of reliably retrieving information from a large memory.

The general reading problem allows the reader to retain quantum information between successive interactions with the memory. Ref.~\cite{DW19} formulated quantum reading protocols that include arbitrary adaptive operations between channel uses and established converse bounds for this setting. This paper also presented bounds for environment-parametrized memory cells and evaluated capacities for several structured families. Other work has shown that entanglement across probe inputs can increase achievable reading rates~\cite{LP17}.  These results illustrate why determining the capacity requires controlling a substantially larger class of strategies than independent probing followed by separate measurements.

When quantum memories are probed by photonic systems, such as the ones considered in~\cite{Pirandola11,PLGMB11}, it becomes necessary to introduce energy constraints on the probe states to ensure the reading rate can be achieved by physical processes. Similar energy constraints were also considered in~\cite{DW19}. In this work, we require the reading protocol to follow an average energy constraint, where the average energy of the probe states per memory cell cannot exceed a fixed energy budget. This allows the maximum flexibility to the decoder, where they can allocate large energy to read some memory cells by reducing the energy allocation for reading other memory cells.

Existing information-theoretic bounds do not, by themselves, provide a general numerical procedure for evaluating the energy-constrained capacity. In particular, the general adaptive converse of~\cite{DW19}  involves an optimization of an information gain over input ensembles and a reference system whose dimension is not bounded in its formulation. Bounds based on particular channel simulations can be effective when a suitable simulation is available, but they do not directly supply a uniform computational prescription for an arbitrary finite channel family. Likewise, a non-adaptive achievable rate can be evaluated for a fixed probe and encoding distribution, while jointly optimizing these choices remains a separate numerical problem. These considerations motivate bounds that combine operational validity with explicit finite-dimensional optimization procedures.

\subsection{Summary of results}

In this work, we derive an upper bound on the energy-constrained reading capacity of an arbitrary finite family of finite-dimensional quantum channels. The bound applies to the general adaptive protocols described above. Our approach uses an input-dependent chain rule for the Belavkin--Staszewski relative entropy~\cite{HLSW26} to bound the information accumulated over the entire protocol. Expressing the resulting quantity through the channel Choi operators converts the average-energy constraint into an operator inequality involving the Hamiltonian. Semidefinite approximations of the operator relative entropy~\cite{FS19} then provide a means of evaluating the bound numerically. In the absence of an energy constraint, the converse reduces to an information radius defined by the Belavkin--Staszewski channel relative entropy.

We also develop a numerical approach to the familiar non-adaptive achievable rate obtained by probing each cell with the same state and jointly decoding the outputs. Using an integral representation of the Umegaki relative entropy~\cite{Jen24} and its semidefinite discretizations~\cite{KS26,KW26}, we construct a family of bilinear semidefinite optimization problems that lower-bound this rate. Each feasible solution provides a probe state and an encoding distribution, whose Holevo information can then be evaluated directly. Alternating optimization offers a practical way to search for such solutions, with each block update given by a semidefinite program. This procedure does not require finding the global optimum in order to produce an achievable rate.

For jointly classical--quantum channels, in which every channel measures its input in the same orthonormal basis, we obtain a converse based directly on the Umegaki relative entropy. Its derivation uses the input-dependent form of the classical--quantum channel chain rule~\cite{WBHK20}. Without an energy constraint, this converse matches a non-adaptive achievable rate and yields an information-radius characterization of the capacity. In particular, we recover a recent result of~\cite{Pascual26,PW26} that the unconstrained reading capacity of a set of jointly classical--quantum channels is achieved by a non-adaptive protocol.

\subsection{Paper organization}

The remainder of this paper is organized as follows: Section~\ref{sec:notations} introduces the notation and relative entropies used throughout the work. Section~\ref{sec:read_cap_defn} defines quantum reading capacity with energy constraints. Section~\ref{sec:read_cap_bnd} establishes the converse and achievable bounds and addresses the task of quantum reading with jointly classical--quantum channels, and Section~\ref{sec:numerics} presents numerical examples.

\section{Notation and Preliminaries}\label{sec:notations}

In this section, we review background material on the three major elements that we use in the rest of the work: quantum states, channels, and divergences.

\subsection{Quantum states and channels}

A quantum state $\rho_A$ is a positive semidefinite, unit-trace operator acting on a Hilbert space~$\mathcal{H}_A$. We denote the set of all linear operators acting on the Hilbert space $\mathcal{H}_A$ by $\mathcal{L}(A)$ and the set of all quantum states acting on this Hilbert space by $\mathcal{S}(A)$. A bipartite quantum state $\rho_{AB}$ acting on the Hilbert space $\mathcal{H}_{A}\otimes \mathcal{H}_B$ is called separable if it can be written as
\begin{equation}
	\rho_{AB} = \sum_{x\in \mathcal{X}} p(x)\sigma^x_{A}\otimes\tau^x_{B},
 \label{eq:def-sep-state}
\end{equation}
where $\{p(x)\}_{x\in \mathcal{X}}$ is a probability distribution and $\{\sigma^x_{A}\}_{x\in \mathcal{X}}$ and $\{\tau^x_{B}\}_{x\in \mathcal{X}}$ are sets of states. Any quantum state that is not separable is said to be entangled. The maximally entangled state vector in the Hilbert space $\mathcal{H}_{A}\otimes\mathcal{H}_B$ is denoted as follows:
\begin{equation}
	|\Phi^d\rangle_{AB} \coloneqq \frac{1}{\sqrt{d}}\sum_{i=0}^{d-1} |i\rangle_A|i\rangle_B,
\end{equation}
where $\{|i\rangle\}_{i=0}^{d-1}$ is an orthonormal basis and $d$ is the Schmidt rank of the state. We denote the corresponding density operator as $\Phi^d_{AB} \equiv |\Phi^d\rangle\!\langle \Phi^d|_{AB}$. 

A quantum channel $\mathcal{N}_{A\to B}$ is a completely positive (CP) and trace-preserving (TP) linear map that takes an operator acting on the Hilbert space $\mathcal{H}_A$ as input and outputs an operator acting on the Hilbert space $\mathcal{H}_B$. We denote the Choi operator of a channel $\mathcal{N}_{A\to B}$ by  $\Gamma^{\mathcal{N}}_{RB}$, which is defined as follows:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:Choi_op_defn}
	\Gamma^{\mathcal{N}}_{RB} \coloneqq \mathcal{N}_{A\to B}\!\left(d\Phi^d_{RA}\right),
\end{equation}
where $\Phi^d_{RA}$ is the maximally entangled state of Schmidt rank $d$ and system $R$ is isomorphic to system $A$. The normalized Choi operator is called the Choi state of the channel, and it is defined as follows:
\begin{equation}
    \Phi^{\mathcal{N}}_{RB} \coloneqq \mathcal{N}_{A\to B}\!\left(\Phi^d_{RA}\right).
\end{equation}

For a bipartite state $\rho_{AB}$, we use the notation $\rho_A \coloneqq \operatorname{Tr}_B\!\left[\rho_{AB}\right]$.

\subsection{Quantum relative entropies and their identities}

The generalized divergence of states and channels plays an important role in our investigation. 

Let $\mathbb{R}$ denote the field of real numbers. A generalized divergence of states~\cite{PV10} is a functional $\mathbf{D}\colon \mathcal{S}(A)\times \mathcal{S}(A) \to \mathbb{R}\cup \{+\infty\}$, such that, for arbitrary states $\rho_A,\sigma_A\in \mathcal{S}(A)$ and an arbitrary channel $\mathcal{N}_{A\to B}$, the data-processing inequality holds
\begin{equation}
    \mathbf{D}\!\left(\rho_A\Vert\sigma_A\right) \ge \mathbf{D}\!\left(\mathcal{N}_{A\to B}(\rho_A)\Vert\mathcal{N}_{A\to B}(\sigma_A)\right).
\end{equation}

Let us briefly discuss some exemplary generalized divergences and their properties that are relevant to this work. 

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Hypothesis-testing relative entropy:} The hypothesis-testing relative entropy between two states is defined for a parameter $\varepsilon \in [0,1]$ as follows~\cite{BD10,BD11,WR12}:
    \begin{equation}\label{eq:hypo_test_rel_ent_defn}
    D^{\varepsilon}_H\!\left(\rho\Vert\sigma\right) \coloneqq -\log_2 \inf_{0\le \Lambda \le I}\left\{\operatorname{Tr}\!\left[\Lambda\sigma\right]:\operatorname{Tr}\!\left[\Lambda\rho\right]\ge 1-\varepsilon\right\}.
\end{equation}

\item \textbf{Umegaki relative entropy:} The Umegaki relative entropy, also known as quantum relative entropy, is defined as follows~\cite{Ume62}:
\begin{equation}
    D\!\left(\rho\Vert\sigma\right) \coloneqq \Bigg\{\begin{array}{cc}
            \operatorname{Tr}\!\left[\rho\!\left(\log_2\rho - \log_2\sigma\right)\right] & \text{if }\operatorname{supp}(\rho) \subseteq \operatorname{supp}(\sigma)   \\
             +\infty & \text{otherwise} 
        \end{array},
\end{equation}
where the logarithm is taken on the support of its argument.

The Umegaki relative entropy between two states $\rho$ and $\sigma$ is related to the hypothesis-testing relative entropy as follows~\cite{WR12}:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:hypo_test_vs_umegaki}
    D^{\varepsilon}_H\!\left(\rho\middle\Vert\sigma\right) \le \frac{1}{1-\varepsilon}\!\left(D\!\left(\rho\middle\Vert\sigma\right) + h_2(\varepsilon)\right) \qquad \forall \varepsilon\in[0,1),
\end{equation}
where $h_2(\varepsilon)\coloneqq -\varepsilon\log_2(\varepsilon) - (1-\varepsilon)\log_2(1-\varepsilon)$ is the binary entropy.

The Umegaki relative entropy has the direct-sum property~\cite[Exercise 11.8.8]{Wilde17book}. Let $p\colon \mathcal{X} \to [0,1]$ be a probability distribution over a finite-dimensional alphabet $\mathcal{X}$. Let $\left\{\rho^x_A\right\}_{x\in \mathcal{X}}$ and $\left\{\sigma^x_A\right\}_{x\in \mathcal{X}}$ be sets of states. Then,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:Umegaki_direct_sum}
    D\!\left(\sum_{x\in \mathcal{X}}p(x)|x\rangle\!\langle x|_X\otimes \rho^x_A\middle\Vert\sum_{x\in \mathcal{X}}p(x)|x\rangle\!\langle x|_X\otimes \sigma^x_A\right) = \sum_{x\in \mathcal{X}}p(x)D\!\left(\rho^x_A\Vert\sigma^x_A\right).
\end{equation}

\item \textbf{Belavkin--Staszewski relative entropy:} The Belavkin--Staszewski relative entropy of states is defined as follows~\cite{BS82}:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:BS_defn}
        \widehat{D}\!\left(\rho\Vert\sigma\right) \coloneqq \Bigg\{\begin{array}{cc}
            \operatorname{Tr}\!\left[\rho\log_2\!\left(\sqrt{\rho}\sigma^{-1}\sqrt{\rho}\right)\right] & \text{if }\operatorname{supp}(\rho) \subseteq \operatorname{supp}(\sigma)   \\
             +\infty & \text{otherwise} 
        \end{array},
    \end{equation}
    where $\sigma^{-1}$ is taken on the support of $\sigma$ and the logarithm is evaluated on the support of $\rho$.

    The Belavkin--Staszewski relative entropy of a pair of states $\rho$ and $\sigma$ is bounded from below by the Umegaki relative entropy \cite{HiaiPetz1993GoldenThompson}. That is,
    \begin{equation}\label{eq:BS_ge_Umegaki}
        \widehat{D}(\rho\Vert\sigma) \ge D(\rho\Vert\sigma).
    \end{equation}



\end{itemize}

The generalized divergence of channels is defined in terms of the generalized divergence of states as follows \cite{LKDW18}:
\begin{equation}
    \mathbf{D}\!\left(\mathcal{N}_{A\to B}\middle\Vert\mathcal{M}_{A\to B}\right) \coloneqq \sup_{\rho_{RA}}\mathbf{D}\!\left(\mathcal{N}_{A\to B}\!\left(\rho_{RA}\right)\middle\Vert\mathcal{M}_{A\to B}\!\left(\rho_{RA}\right)\right),
    \label{eq:def-gen-div}
\end{equation}
where the supremum is taken over every state $\rho_{RA}$ with no restriction on the dimension of $R$.

The Belavkin--Staszewski relative entropy of channels is defined as an instance of \eqref{eq:def-gen-div} as follows:
\begin{equation}
    \widehat{D}\!\left(\mathcal{N}_{A\to B}\middle\Vert\mathcal{M}_{A\to B}\right) \coloneqq \sup_{\rho_{RA}}\widehat{D}\!\left(\mathcal{N}_{A\to B}\!\left(\rho_{RA}\right)\middle\Vert\mathcal{M}_{A\to B}\!\left(\rho_{RA}\right)\right),
\end{equation}
has the following chain-rule property~\cite{FF21}:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:geo_ent_amort}
    \widehat{D}\!\left(\mathcal{N}\!\left(\rho\right)\middle\Vert\mathcal{M}\!\left(\sigma\right)\right) \le \widehat{D}\!\left(\mathcal{N}\middle\Vert\mathcal{M}\right) + \widehat{D}\!\left(\rho\Vert\sigma\right).
\end{equation}

A convenient way to study the Belavkin--Staszewski relative entropy of channels is through the operator relative entropy \cite{Furuta1990Operator}, which is defined as follows:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:D_op_defn}
    \widehat{D}_{\operatorname{op}}\!\left(X,Y\right) \coloneqq X^{\frac{1}{2}}\log_2\!\left(X^{\frac{1}{2}}Y^{-1}X^{\frac{1}{2}}\right)X^{\frac{1}{2}} \qquad \operatorname{if }\operatorname{supp}\!\left(X\right) \subseteq \operatorname{supp}(Y),
\end{equation}
where the inverse is taken on the support of $Y$.
It was shown in~\cite[Theorem~2]{FF21} that the Belavkin--Staszewski relative entropy of channels can be expressed in the following closed form:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:BS_ch_closed_form}
    \widehat{D}\!\left(\mathcal{N}_{A\to B}\middle\Vert\mathcal{M}_{A\to B}\right) = \left\Vert\operatorname{Tr}_B\!\left[\widehat{D}_{\operatorname{op}}\!\left(\Gamma^{\mathcal{N}}_{AB},\Gamma^{\mathcal{M}}_{AB}\right)\right]\right\Vert_{\infty},
\end{equation}
where $\Gamma^{\mathcal{N}}$ and $\Gamma^{\mathcal{M}}$ are the Choi operators of the channels $\mathcal{N}$ and $\mathcal{M}$, respectively.

The following input-dependent chain rule for the Belavkin--Staszewski relative entropy in terms of the operator relative entropy was obtained in~\cite[Appendix B]{HLSW26}: 
\begin{equation}\label{eq:ec_chain_rule_ing}
    \widehat{D}\!\left(\mathcal{N}_{A\to B}\!\left(\rho_{RA}\right)\Vert\mathcal{M}_{A\to B}\!\left(\sigma_{RA}\right)\right) \le \widehat{D}\!\left(\rho_{RA}\Vert\sigma_{RA}\right) + \operatorname{Tr}\!\left[\left(\rho^T_A\otimes I_B\right)\widehat{D}_{\operatorname{op}}\!\left(\Gamma^{\mathcal{N}}_{AB},\Gamma^{\mathcal{M}}_{AB}\right)\right]
\end{equation}
if $\operatorname{supp}\!\left(\Gamma^{\mathcal{N}}_{AB}\right) \subseteq \operatorname{supp}\!\left(\Gamma^{\mathcal{M}}_{AB}\right)$, where $\rho^T_A$ denotes the transpose of $\rho_A$ with respect to the chosen basis for system $A$ in the representation of the Choi operators $\Gamma^{\mathcal{N}}$ and $\Gamma^{\mathcal{M}}$.

\section{Reading capacity of channels}\label{sec:read_cap_defn}

In this section, we describe the task of quantum reading and define the energy-constrained reading capacity of a set of channels, which is the quantity we investigate in the remainder of this paper. We follow~\cite{DW19} for our definition of the energy-constrained reading capacity.

In a quantum reading protocol, an encoder has the ability to inscribe a memory cell with a quantum channel selected from a set of channels $\left\{\mathcal{N}^{x}_{A\to B}\right\}_{x\in \mathcal{X}}$. The encoder encodes a message $m\in \mathcal{M}$ in the quantum memory using a codeword $x^n(m)\coloneqq [x_1(m),x_2(m),\ldots,x_n(m)]$ such that the $i^{\text{th}}$ memory cell is inscribed by the channel $\mathcal{N}^{x_i(m)}_{A\to B}$. The decoder then uses a probe state $\rho_{AR_1}$, a set of intermediate processing channels $\left\{\mathcal{A}^i_{BR_i\to AR_{i+1}}\right\}_{i\in [n-1]}$, and a decoding POVM $\left\{\Lambda^{\hat{m}}_{BR_n}\right\}_{\hat{m}\in \mathcal{M}}$ to retrieve the original message $m$, where systems $R_1, R_2,\ldots, R_n$ are of arbitrary dimension. 

Let us denote the set of channels, $\left\{\mathcal{N}^{x}_{A\to B}\right\}_{x\in \mathcal{X}}$, by the symbol $\mathcal{N}$. Let us also define the following set that describes a reading protocol:
\begin{equation}
    \mathcal{P}^{(n)}\coloneqq \left\{\rho_{AR_1},\left\{\mathcal{A}^i_{BR_i\to AR_{i+1}}\right\}_{i\in [n-1]},\left\{\Lambda^{\hat{m}}_{BR_n}\right\}_{\hat{m}\in \mathcal{M}}\right\}.
\end{equation}
The error in the decoding an arbitrary message $m$ encoded in $n$ channels using a protocol $\mathcal{P}^{(n)}$ is defined as follows:
\begin{equation}
    p_{\operatorname{err}}\!\left(\mathcal{N},\mathcal{P}^{(n)}\right) \coloneqq \max_{m\in \mathcal{M}}\sum_{\hat{m}\in \mathcal{M}\setminus \{m\}}\operatorname{Tr}\!\left[\Lambda^{\hat{m}}_{BR_n}\left(\mathcal{N}^{x_n(m)}\circ\mathcal{A}^{n-1}\circ\mathcal{N}^{x_{n-1}(m)}\circ\cdots\circ\mathcal{N}^{x_1(m)}\!\left(\rho_{AR_1}\right)\right)\right].
\end{equation}

\begin{definition}
    The $n$-shot reading capacity of a set of channels $\mathcal{N}\coloneqq \left\{\mathcal{N}^{x}_{A\to B}\right\}_{x\in \mathcal{X}}$ is defined as follows:
    \begin{equation}
        R^{\varepsilon,n}\!\left(\mathcal{N}\right) \coloneqq \sup_{\mathcal{P}^{(n)}}\left\{
             \log_2|\mathcal{M}|:  
              p_{\operatorname{err}}\!\left(\mathcal{N},\mathcal{P}^{(n)}\right) \le \varepsilon
        \right\}, 
    \end{equation}
    where 
    \begin{equation}
    \mathcal{P}^{(n)}\coloneqq \left\{\rho_{AR_1},\left\{\mathcal{A}^i_{BR_i\to AR_{i+1}}\right\}_{i\in [n-1]},\left\{\Lambda^{\hat{m}}_{BR_n}\right\}_{\hat{m}\in \mathcal{M}}\right\}. 
    \label{eq:protocol-P}
    \end{equation}
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}
    The reading capacity of a set of channels $\mathcal{N}$ is defined as follows:
    \begin{equation}
        R\!\left(\mathcal{N}\right) \coloneqq \liminf_{\varepsilon \to 0^+}\liminf_{n\to \infty}\frac{1}{n}R^{\varepsilon, n}\!\left(\mathcal{N}\right).
    \end{equation}
\end{definition}

In practical scenarios, the decoder does not have access to arbitrary probe states and post-processing channels. Instead, the decoder is restricted by some energy constraint. In light of this practical restriction, we define the energy-constrained $n$-shot reading capacity of a set of channels, where the average energy of the states used to probe each channel cannot exceed a given threshold. Consider a reading protocol $\mathcal{P}^{(n)}$ of the form in \eqref{eq:protocol-P}. The state that probes the $i^{\operatorname{th}}$ channel in this reading protocol is given by the following:
\begin{equation}
    \rho^{i-1,m}_{AR_{i}} \coloneqq  \mathcal{A}^{i-1}_{BR_{i-1}\to AR_{i}}\circ\mathcal{N}^{x_{i-1}(m)}_{A\to B}\!\left(\rho^{i-2,m}_{AR_{i-1}}\right) \qquad \forall i\in \left\{2,3,\ldots,n\right\}, m\in \mathcal{M}
\end{equation}
where $\rho^{0,m}_{AR_1} = \rho_{AR_1}$. Let $H_A$ be the Hamiltonian associated with the probing system, and let $E$ be the average-energy threshold. Then the $n$-shot reading capacity of a set of channels $\mathcal{N}$ is defined as follows:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:n_shot_ec_read_cap_defn}
        R^{\varepsilon,n}_{H,E}\!\left(\mathcal{N}\right) \coloneqq \sup_{\mathcal{P}^{(n)}}\left\{
             \log_2|\mathcal{M}|:  
              p_{\operatorname{err}}\!\left(\mathcal{N},\mathcal{P}^{(n)}\right) \le \varepsilon, \operatorname{Tr}\!\left[H_A\overline{\rho}^m_A\right]\le E \quad \forall m\in\mathcal{M}
        \right\}, 
\end{equation}
where 
\begin{equation}\label{eq:avg_state_defn}
    \overline{\rho}^m_A \coloneqq \frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=0}^{n-1}\operatorname{Tr}_{R_{i+1}}\!\left[\rho^{i,m}_{AR_{i+1}}\right] \qquad \forall m\in \mathcal{M}.
\end{equation}
The energy-constrained reading capacity of the set of channels $\mathcal{N}$, for a given Hamiltonian $H$ and average-energy threshold $E$, is then  defined as follows:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:ec_read_cap_defn}
        R_{H,E}\!\left(\mathcal{N}\right) \coloneqq \liminf_{\varepsilon \to 0^+}\liminf_{n\to \infty}\frac{1}{n}R^{\varepsilon, n}_{H,E}\!\left(\mathcal{N}\right).
\end{equation}

\section{Efficiently computable bounds on the energy-constrained reading capacity}\label{sec:read_cap_bnd}

In this section, we derive efficiently computable upper and lower bounds on the energy-constrained reading capacity of a set of channels. Here, we use the term efficiently computable to indicate that the bounds can be computed in $O(\text{poly}(d))$, where $d$ is the dimension of the Choi operators of the channels.

\subsection{Upper bound on the energy-constrained reading capacity}

Let us first consider an upper bound on the energy-constrained reading capacity from above. Our first main result is encapsulated in Theorem~\ref{theo:ec_read_cap_ub} stated below.

\begin{theorem}\label{theo:ec_read_cap_ub}
    The reading capacity of a set of channels $\mathcal{N}\coloneqq \left\{\mathcal{N}^x_{A\to B}\right\}_{x\in \mathcal{X}}$ is bounded from above as follows:
    \begin{equation}\label{eq:ec_read_cap_ub}
    R_{H,E}\!\left(\mathcal{N}\right) \le \inf_{\Gamma^{\mathcal{S}}_{AB}\ge 0}\inf_{\lambda \in \mathbb{R},\mu\ge 0}\left\{
    \begin{array}{c}
         \lambda + \mu E: \\
         \left(\operatorname{Tr}_B\!\left[\widehat{D}_{\operatorname{op}}\!\left(\Gamma^{x}_{AB},\Gamma^{\mathcal{S}}_{AB}\right)\right]\right)^T \le \lambda I + \mu H \qquad \forall x\in \mathcal{X},\\
         \operatorname{Tr}_B\!\left[\Gamma^{\mathcal{S}}_{AB}\right] = I_A
    \end{array}
    \right\},
\end{equation}
where $\Gamma^x_{AB}$ is the Choi operator of $\mathcal{N}^x_{A\to B}$ for every $x\in \mathcal{X}$ and $\widehat{D}_{\operatorname{op}}$ is defined in~\eqref{eq:D_op_defn}.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
    Let $\left\{\mathcal{N}^{x}_{A\to B}\right\}_{x\in \mathcal{X}}$ be a set of channels, and let $\left\{x^n(m)\right\}_{m\in \mathcal{M}_n}$ be a codebook that is being used to encode a message $m$ into $n$ channels. Consider an arbitrary quantum reading protocol with probe state $\rho_{AR_1}$, intermediate processing channels $\left\{\mathcal{A}^i_{BR_i\to AR_{i+1}}\right\}_{i\in [n-1]}$, and POVM $\left\{\Lambda^{\hat{m}}_{BR_n}\right\}_{\hat{m}\in \mathcal{M}_n}$ such that the average-energy constraint is satisfied  with respect to the Hamiltonian~$H$ and energy $E$ (see~\eqref{eq:n_shot_ec_read_cap_defn} and~\eqref{eq:avg_state_defn}). We will use the following notations throughout the proof for simplicity:
    \begin{align}
        \mathcal{N} &\coloneqq \left\{\mathcal{N}^x_{A\to B}\right\}_{x\in \mathcal{X}},\\
        \mathcal{P}^{(n)} &\coloneqq \left\{\rho_{AR_1},\left\{\mathcal{A}^i_{BR_i\to AR_{i+1}}\right\}_{i\in [n-1]}, \left\{\Lambda^{\hat{m}}_{BR_n}\right\}_{\hat{m}\in \mathcal{M}_n}\right\}.
    \end{align}

    Let us define the following measurement channel:
    \begin{equation}
        \mathcal{L}_{BR_n\to \hat{M}}(\cdot) \coloneqq \sum_{\hat{m}\in \mathcal{M}_n}\operatorname{Tr}\!\left[\Lambda^{\hat{m}}_{BR_n}(\cdot)\right]|\hat{m}\rangle\!\langle \hat{m}|_{\hat{M}}.
    \end{equation}
    The worst-case error of this reading protocol is then given by the following:
    \begin{equation}
        p_{\operatorname{err}}\!\left(\mathcal{N}, \mathcal{P}^{(n)}\right) = \max_{m\in \mathcal{M}_n}\sum_{\hat{m}\in \mathcal{M}_n\setminus \{m\}}\langle \hat{m}|\mathcal{L}\circ\mathcal{N}^{x_n(m)}\circ\mathcal{A}^{n-1}\circ\cdots\circ\mathcal{N}^{x_1(m)}\!\left(\rho_{AR_1}\right)|\hat{m}\rangle,
    \end{equation}
    and the average case error of this reading protocol is given by the following:
    \begin{equation}\label{eq:avg_case_err_exp}
        \overline{p}_{\operatorname{err}}\!\left(\mathcal{N},\mathcal{P}^{(n)}\right) = \frac{1}{|\mathcal{M}_n|}\sum_{\substack{m,\hat{m}\in \mathcal{M}_n,\\ \hat{m}\neq m}}\langle \hat{m}|\mathcal{L}\circ\mathcal{N}^{x_n(m)}\circ\mathcal{A}^{n-1}\circ\cdots\circ\mathcal{N}^{x_1(m)}\!\left(\rho_{AR_1}\right)|\hat{m}\rangle.
    \end{equation}
    Now consider the following channel:
    \begin{equation}
        \mathcal{Q}^{\mathcal{N}}_{M\to \hat{M}}(\cdot) \coloneqq \sum_{m\in \mathcal{M}_n}\mathcal{L}\circ\mathcal{N}^{x_n(m)}\circ\mathcal{A}^{n-1}\circ\cdots\circ\mathcal{N}^{x_1(m)}\!\left(\rho_{AR_1}\right)\langle m|(\cdot)|m\rangle .
    \end{equation}
    Let $\omega_{MM'}$ denote the maximally-classically correlated state, and let $\Pi_{MM'}$ denote the test for classical correlations. That is,
    \begin{align}
        \omega_{MM'} &\coloneqq \frac{1}{|\mathcal{M}_n|}\sum_{m\in \mathcal{M}_n}|m\rangle\!\langle m|_{M}\otimes |m\rangle\!\langle m|_{M'},\\
        \Pi_{MM'} &\coloneqq \sum_{m\in \mathcal{M}_n}|m\rangle\!\langle m|_{M}\otimes |m\rangle\!\langle m|_{M'}.
    \end{align}
    Let us consider the following expression:
    \begin{align}
        &\operatorname{Tr}\!\left[\Pi_{M'\hat{M}}\mathcal{Q}^{\mathcal{N}}_{M\to \hat{M}}\!\left(\omega_{MM'}\right)\right]\notag\\ &= \frac{1}{|\mathcal{M}_n|}\sum_{m\in \mathcal{M}_n}\operatorname{Tr}\!\left[|m\rangle\!\langle m|_{M'}\langle m|_{\hat{M}}\mathcal{L}\circ\mathcal{N}^{x_n(m)}\circ\mathcal{A}^{n-1}\circ\cdots\circ\mathcal{N}^{x_1(m)}\!\left(\rho_{AR_1}\right)|m\rangle_{\hat{M}}\right]\\
        &= \frac{1}{|\mathcal{M}_n|}\sum_{m\in \mathcal{M}_n}\langle m|_{\hat{M}}\mathcal{L}\circ\mathcal{N}^{x_n(m)}\circ\mathcal{A}^{n-1}\circ\cdots\circ\mathcal{N}^{x_1(m)}\!\left(\rho_{AR_1}\right)|m\rangle_{\hat{M}}\label{eq:avg_succ_prob_proj}\\
        &= 1-\overline{p}_{\operatorname{err}}\!\left(\mathcal{N},\mathcal{P}^{(n)}\right),\label{eq:avg_succ_prob_err_inv}
    \end{align}
    where the first equality follows from simply substituting the expressions for $\Pi_{M'\hat{M}}$, $\omega_{MM'}$, and $\mathcal{Q}^{\mathcal{N}}_{M\to \hat{M}}$, and the last equality follows from~\eqref{eq:avg_case_err_exp}. 

    If the set $\mathcal{N}$ contains only one channel, then it is clearly useless for the task of reading. Let us define
    \begin{equation}
        \mathcal{Q}^{\mathcal{N}_S}_{M\to \hat{M}}(\cdot) \coloneqq \sum_{m\in \mathcal{M}_n}\mathcal{L}\circ\mathcal{S}\circ\mathcal{A}^{n-1}\circ\cdots\circ\mathcal{S}\!\left(\rho_{AR_1}\right)\langle m|(\cdot)|m\rangle,
    \end{equation}
    where $\mathcal{S}_{A\to B}$ is a fixed channel such that $\operatorname{supp}(\Gamma^{\mathcal{N}^x}_{AB})\subseteq \operatorname{supp}(\Gamma^{\mathcal{S}}_{AB})$ for every $x\in \mathcal{X}$. Then,
   \begin{align}
        \operatorname{Tr}\!\left[\Pi_{M'\hat{M}}\mathcal{Q}^{\mathcal{N}_S}_{M\to \hat{M}}\!\left(\omega_{MM'}\right)\right]
        &= \frac{1}{|\mathcal{M}_n|}\sum_{m\in \mathcal{M}_n}\langle m|_{\hat{M}}\mathcal{L}\circ\mathcal{S}\circ\mathcal{A}^{n-1}\circ\cdots\mathcal{A}^{1}\circ\mathcal{S}\!\left(\rho_{AR_1}\right)|m\rangle_{\hat{M}}\\
        &= \frac{1}{|\mathcal{M}_n|}\operatorname{Tr}\!\left[\mathcal{L}\circ\mathcal{S}\circ\mathcal{A}^{n-1}\circ\cdots\mathcal{A}^{1}\circ\mathcal{S}\!\left(\rho_{AR_1}\right)\right]\\
        &= \frac{1}{|\mathcal{M}_n|},\label{eq:corr_test_pass_prob_useless}
    \end{align}
    where the first equality follows from~\eqref{eq:avg_succ_prob_proj}, the second and third equalities follow from the fact that $\mathcal{L}\circ\mathcal{S}\circ\mathcal{A}^{n-1}\circ\cdots\mathcal{A}^{1}\circ\mathcal{S}\!\left(\rho_{AR_1}\right)$ is a quantum state that is independent of the label $m$.

    The equality in~\eqref{eq:avg_succ_prob_err_inv} implies that for every set of channels $\mathcal{N}$ and reading protocol $\mathcal{P}^{(n)}$, if the worst case error in reading is less than or equal to $\varepsilon$ for some $\varepsilon\in (0,1)$, then
    \begin{equation}\label{eq:corr_test_pass_prob_res}
        \operatorname{Tr}\!\left[\Pi_{M'\hat{M}}\mathcal{Q}^{\mathcal{N}}_{M\to \hat{M}}\!\left(\omega_{MM'}\right)\right] \ge 1-\varepsilon
    \end{equation}
    since $\overline{p}_{\operatorname{err}}(\mathcal{N},\mathcal{P}^{(n)}) \le p_{\operatorname{err}}(\mathcal{N},\mathcal{P}^{(n)})$. Recall the definition of hypothesis testing relative entropy from~\eqref{eq:hypo_test_rel_ent_defn}. Since $0\le \Pi_{M'\hat{M}}\le I_{M'\hat{M}}$, \eqref{eq:corr_test_pass_prob_useless} and~\eqref{eq:corr_test_pass_prob_res} imply that
    \begin{equation}\label{eq:n_mess_hypo_test_ub}
        D^{\varepsilon}_H\!\left(\mathcal{Q}^{\mathcal{N}}_{M\to \hat{M}}\!\left(\omega_{MM'}\right)\middle\Vert\mathcal{Q}^{\mathcal{N}_S}_{M\to \hat{M}}\!\left(\omega_{MM'}\right)\right) \ge -\log_2\frac{1}{|\mathcal{M}_n|} = \log_2|\mathcal{M}_n|.
    \end{equation}
    Let us define the following states:
    \begin{align}
        \sigma^{(m)}_{\hat{M}}&\coloneqq \mathcal{L}\circ\mathcal{N}^{x_n(m)}\circ\mathcal{A}^{n-1}\circ\cdots\circ\mathcal{N}^{x_1(m)}\!\left(\rho_{AR_1}\right)\\
        \tau_{\hat{M}}&\coloneqq \mathcal{L}\circ\mathcal{S}\circ\mathcal{A}^{n-1}\circ\cdots\circ\mathcal{S}\!\left(\rho_{AR_1}\right).
    \end{align}
    Then,
    \begin{align}
        \mathcal{Q}^{\mathcal{N}}_{M\to \hat{M}}\!\left(\omega_{MM'}\right) &= \frac{1}{|\mathcal{M}_n|}\sum_{m\in \mathcal{M}_n}|m\rangle\!\langle m|_{M'}\otimes\sigma^{(m)}_{\hat{M}}\\
        \mathcal{Q}^{\mathcal{N}_S}_{M\to \hat{M}}\!\left(\omega_{MM'}\right) &= \frac{1}{|\mathcal{M}_n|}\sum_{m\in \mathcal{M}_n}|m\rangle\!\langle m|_{M'}\otimes\tau_{\hat{M}}
    \end{align}
    The inequality in~\eqref{eq:n_mess_hypo_test_ub} then leads to the following:
    \begin{align}
        &\log_2|\mathcal{M}_n|\notag\\
        &\le D^{\varepsilon}_H\!\left(\frac{1}{|\mathcal{M}_n|}\sum_{m\in \mathcal{M}_n}|m\rangle\!\langle m|_{M'}\otimes\sigma^{(m)}_{\hat{M}}\middle\Vert\frac{1}{|\mathcal{M}_n|}\sum_{m\in \mathcal{M}_n}|m\rangle\!\langle m|_{M'}\otimes\tau_{\hat{M}}\right)\\
        &\le \frac{1}{1-\varepsilon}\!\left(D\!\left(\frac{1}{|\mathcal{M}_n|}\sum_{m\in \mathcal{M}_n}|m\rangle\!\langle m|_{M'}\otimes\sigma^{(m)}_{\hat{M}}\middle\Vert\frac{1}{|\mathcal{M}_n|}\sum_{m\in \mathcal{M}_n}|m\rangle\!\langle m|_{M'}\otimes\tau_{\hat{M}}\right) + h_2(\varepsilon)\right)\\
        &= \frac{1}{1-\varepsilon}\!\left(\frac{1}{\left|\mathcal{M}_n\right|}\sum_{m\in \mathcal{M}_n}D\!\left(\sigma^{(m)}_{\hat{M}}\middle\Vert\tau_{\hat{M}}\right) + h_2(\varepsilon)\right)\\
        &\le \max_{m\in \mathcal{M}_n}\frac{1}{1-\varepsilon}\!\left(D\!\left(\sigma^{(m)}_{\hat{M}}\middle\Vert\tau_{\hat{M}}\right) + h_2(\varepsilon)\right),\label{eq:n_mess_umegaki_ub}
    \end{align}
where the second inequality follows from~\eqref{eq:hypo_test_vs_umegaki}, the equality follows from~\eqref{eq:Umegaki_direct_sum}, and the final inequality follows because the average is less than or equal to the maximum of a set of numbers. Since the second term in~\eqref{eq:n_mess_umegaki_ub} is independent of the number of channel uses $n$, the following inequality holds for every $\varepsilon\in [0,1)$:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:read_rate_ub_Umegaki}
    \liminf_{n\to \infty}\frac{\log_2|\mathcal{M}_n|}{n} \le \liminf_{n\to \infty}\max_{m\in \mathcal{M}_n}\frac{1}{n(1-\varepsilon)}D\!\left(\sigma^{(m)}_{\hat{M}}\middle\Vert\tau_{\hat{M}}\right).
\end{equation}
Note that $\sigma^{(m)}_{\hat{M}}$ and $\tau_{\hat{M}}$ implicitly depend on $n$.

Let us define the following states:
\begin{align}
    \sigma^{i,m}_{AR_{i+1}} &\coloneqq \mathcal{A}^i_{BR_{i}\to AR_{i+1}}\circ\mathcal{N}^{x_i(m)}_{A\to B}\!\left(\sigma^{i-1,m}_{AR_i}\right) \qquad \forall i\in [n-1],\\
    \tau^i_{AR_{i+1}} &\coloneqq \mathcal{A}^i_{BR_{i}\to AR_{i+1}}\circ\mathcal{S}_{A\to B}\!\left(\tau^{i-1}_{AR_i}\right) \qquad \forall i\in [n-1],\\
    \sigma^{n,m}_{BR_n} &\coloneqq \mathcal{N}^{x_n(m)}_{A\to B}\!\left(\sigma^{n-1,m}_{AR_n}\right),\\
    \tau^{n}_{BR_n} &\coloneqq \mathcal{S}_{A\to B}\!\left(\tau^{n-1}_{AR_n}\right),
\end{align}
where
\begin{equation}
    \sigma^{0,m}_{AR_1} = \tau^0_{AR_1} = \rho_{AR_1}.
\end{equation}
Let us denote the Choi operator of $\mathcal{N}^{x_n(m)}$ by $\Gamma^{x_n(m)}_{AB}$ and the Choi operator of $\mathcal{S}_{A\to B}$ by $\Gamma^{\mathcal{S}}_{AB}$. Then,
\begin{align}
    D\!\left(\sigma^{(m)}_{\hat{M}}\middle\Vert\tau_{\hat{M}}\right) &= D\!\left(\mathcal{L}_{BR_n\to \hat{M}}\!\left(\sigma^{n,m}_{BR_n}\right)\middle\Vert\mathcal{L}_{BR_n\to \hat{M}}\!\left(\tau^{n}_{BR_n}\right)\right)\\
    &\le D\!\left(\sigma^{n,m}_{BR_n}\middle\Vert\tau^{n}_{BR_n}\right)\label{eq:Before_umegaki_to_BS}\\
    &\le \widehat{D}\!\left(\sigma^{n,m}_{BR_n}\middle\Vert\tau^{n}_{BR_n}\right)\label{eq:Umegaki_to_BS}\\
    &\le \widehat{D}\!\left(\sigma^{n-1,m}_{AR_n}\middle\Vert\tau^{n-1}_{AR_n}\right) + \operatorname{Tr}\!\left[\left(\operatorname{Tr}_{R_n}\!\left[\sigma^{n-1,m}_{AR_n}\right]\right)^T\widehat{D}_{\operatorname{op}}\!\left(\Gamma^{x_n(m)}_{AB},\Gamma^{\mathcal{S}}_{AB}\right)\right],\label{eq:ec_chain_rule_apply_1_step}
\end{align}
where the first inequality follows from the data-processing inequality for the Umegaki relative entropy, the second inequality follows from~\eqref{eq:BS_ge_Umegaki}, and the last inequality follows from~\eqref{eq:ec_chain_rule_ing}. We can apply~\eqref{eq:ec_chain_rule_ing} to the first term on the right-hand side of~\eqref{eq:ec_chain_rule_apply_1_step} as well. Following this process sequentially and using the data-processing inequality, we arrive at the following:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:BS_rel_ent_le_avg_BS}
    \widehat{D}\!\left(\sigma^{(m)}_{\hat{M}}\middle\Vert\tau_{\hat{M}}\right) \le \sum_{i=0}^{n-1}\operatorname{Tr}\!\left[\left(\operatorname{Tr}_{R_{i+1}}\!\left[\sigma^{i,m}_{AR_{i+1}}\right]\right)^T\widehat{D}_{\operatorname{op}}\!\left(\Gamma^{x_{i+1}(m)}_{AB},\Gamma^{\mathcal{S}}_{AB}\right)\right] 
\end{equation}
Let $Q_A$ be an operator such that
\begin{equation}\label{eq:ub_BS_op_marg}
    Q_A \ge \operatorname{Tr}_B\!\left[\widehat{D}_{\operatorname{op}}\!\left(\Gamma^{x}_{AB},\Gamma^{\mathcal{S}}_{AB}\right)\right] \qquad \forall x\in \mathcal{X}.
\end{equation}
Then,
\begin{align}
    \widehat{D}\!\left(\sigma^{(m)}_{\hat{M}}\middle\Vert\tau_{\hat{M}}\right) &\le n\operatorname{Tr}\!\left[\left(\overline{\sigma}^{(m)}_A\right)^TQ_A\right]\\
    &= n\operatorname{Tr}\!\left[\overline{\sigma}^{(m)}_AQ^T_A\right],
\end{align}
where
\begin{equation}
    \overline{\sigma}^{(m)}_A \coloneqq \frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=0}^{n-1}\operatorname{Tr}_{R_{i+1}}\!\left[\sigma^{i,m}_{AR_{i+1}}\right]
\end{equation}
is the average-input state to read the message $m$ encoded in the channels. Note that the average-energy constraint implies that $\operatorname{Tr}\!\left[H_A\overline{\sigma}^{(m)}_A\right]\le E$ for every $m\in \mathcal{M}_n$. Therefore, for every $n\in \mathbb{N}$, every $m\in \mathcal{M}_n$, and every operator $Q$ that satisfies the inequality in~\eqref{eq:ub_BS_op_marg}, the following inequality holds:
\begin{equation}
     \frac{1}{n}\widehat{D}\!\left(\sigma^{(m)}_{\hat{M}}\middle\Vert\tau_{\hat{M}}\right) \le \sup_{\sigma\ge 0}\left\{\operatorname{Tr}\!\left[\sigma Q^T\right]: \operatorname{Tr}\!\left[\sigma\right] = 1, \operatorname{Tr}\!\left[H\sigma\right]\le E\right\}.\label{eq:}
\end{equation}
The right-hand side of the above inequality is a semidefinite program whose dual can be found using the Lagrange multiplier method as follows:
\begin{align}
    & \sup_{\sigma\ge 0}\left\{\operatorname{Tr}\!\left[\sigma Q^T\right]: \operatorname{Tr}\!\left[\sigma\right] = 1, \operatorname{Tr}\!\left[H\sigma\right]\le E\right\} \notag \\
    &= \sup_{\sigma \ge 0} \operatorname{Tr}\!\left[\sigma Q^T\right] + \inf_{\substack{\lambda \in \mathbb{R},\\ \mu\ge 0}}\lambda\!\left(1-\operatorname{Tr}\!\left[\sigma\right]\right) + \mu\!\left(E - \operatorname{Tr}\!\left[H\sigma\right]\right)\\
    &\le \inf_{\substack{\lambda \in \mathbb{R},\\ \mu\ge 0}}\sup_{\sigma \ge 0}\operatorname{Tr}\!\left[\sigma Q^T\right] + \lambda\!\left(1-\operatorname{Tr}\!\left[\sigma\right]\right) + \mu\!\left(E - \operatorname{Tr}\!\left[H\sigma\right]\right)\\
    &= \inf_{\substack{\lambda \in \mathbb{R},\\ \mu\ge 0}} \lambda + \mu E + \sup_{\sigma \ge 0} \operatorname{Tr}\!\left[\sigma\!\left(Q^T - \lambda I - \mu H\right)\right]\\
    &=\inf_{\lambda \in \mathbb{R},\mu\ge 0}\left\{\lambda + \mu E: Q^T \le \lambda I + \mu H\right\},\label{eq:ec_op_norm_dual}
\end{align}
where the inequality follows from the max-min inequality. As such,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:BS_ub_SDP_form}
    \frac{1}{n}\widehat{D}\!\left(\sigma^{(m)}_{\hat{M}}\middle\Vert\tau_{\hat{M}}\right) \le \inf_{\lambda \in \mathbb{R},\mu\ge 0}\left\{\lambda + \mu E: Q^T \le \lambda I + \mu H\right\} \qquad \forall n\in \mathbb{N},m\in \mathcal{M}_n,
\end{equation}
and consequently,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:Umegaki_ub_SDP_form}
    \frac{1}{n}D\!\left(\sigma^{(m)}_{\hat{M}}\middle\Vert\tau_{\hat{M}}\right) \le \inf_{\lambda \in \mathbb{R},\mu\ge 0}\left\{\lambda + \mu E: Q^T \le \lambda I + \mu H\right\} \qquad \forall n\in \mathbb{N},m\in \mathcal{M}_n.
\end{equation}
Since the transpose map is a positivity preserving map,
\begin{equation}
    \operatorname{Tr}_B\!\left[\widehat{D}_{\operatorname{op}}\!\left(\Gamma^{x}_{AB},\Gamma^{\mathcal{S}}_{AB}\right)\right] \le Q_A \iff \left(\operatorname{Tr}_B\!\left[\widehat{D}_{\operatorname{op}}\!\left(\Gamma^{x}_{AB},\Gamma^{\mathcal{S}}_{AB}\right)\right]\right)^T \le Q^T_A  .
\end{equation}
We can substitute~\eqref{eq:Umegaki_ub_SDP_form} into~\eqref{eq:n_mess_umegaki_ub} and infimize over every channel $\mathcal{S}_{A\to B}$ and every $Q$ that satisfies~\eqref{eq:ub_BS_op_marg} to arrive at the following inequality, which holds for all $n\in \mathbb{N}$ and $\varepsilon\in [0,1)$:
\begin{multline}\label{eq:read_cap_opt_ub_1}
    \frac{\log_2|\mathcal{M}_n|}{n} \le \inf_{Q_A,\Gamma^{\mathcal{S}}_{AB}\ge 0}\inf_{\lambda \in \mathbb{R},\mu\ge 0}\left\{
    \begin{array}{c}
         \frac{1}{1-\varepsilon}\left(\lambda + \mu E + \frac{h_2(\varepsilon)}{n}\right): \\
         \left(\operatorname{Tr}_B\!\left[\widehat{D}_{\operatorname{op}}\!\left(\Gamma^{x}_{AB},\Gamma^{\mathcal{S}}_{AB}\right)\right]\right)^T \le Q^T_A \le \lambda I + \mu H \qquad \forall x\in \mathcal{X},\\
         \operatorname{Tr}_B\!\left[\Gamma^{\mathcal{S}}_{AB}\right] = I_A
    \end{array}
    \right\},
\end{multline}
where we have used the fact that infimizing over channels is equivalent to infimizing over Choi operators due to the Choi isomorphism. Note that we can simply eliminate $Q_A$ in~\eqref{eq:read_cap_opt_ub_1}. Also note that we do not need to include a separate support condition on the Choi operators of $\mathcal{N}^x$ and $\mathcal{S}$. This is because if $\operatorname{supp}\!\left(\Gamma^x_{AB}\right) \not\subseteq \operatorname{supp}\!\left(\Gamma^{\mathcal{S}}\right)$, the value of the objective function in~\eqref{eq:read_cap_opt_ub_1} will be $+\infty$. Therefore, a finite optimal value is achieved only when $\operatorname{supp}\!\left(\Gamma^x_{AB}\right) \subseteq \operatorname{supp}\!\left(\Gamma^{\mathcal{S}}\right)$ for every $x\in \mathcal{X}$.  Finally, acknowledging that the inequality in~\eqref{eq:read_cap_opt_ub_1} holds for every protocol $\mathcal{P}^{(n)}$, every $n\in \mathbb{N}$ and every $\varepsilon \in [0,1)$, and taking the appropriate limits in the definition of the energy-constrained reading capacity of channels given in~\eqref{eq:ec_read_cap_defn}, we conclude the statement of the theorem.
\end{proof}

\medskip

The authors of~\cite{FS19} showed that the operator inequality $\widehat{D}_{\operatorname{op}}(X,Y) \le Q$ can be approximated by a set of semidefinite conditions on $X$, $Y$, and $Q$. This means that the right-hand side of~\eqref{eq:read_cap_opt_ub_1}, which is equal to the right-hand side of~\eqref{eq:ec_read_cap_ub}, can be approximated using a semidefinite program. Thus, we conclude that the upper bound on the energy-constrained reading capacity stated in Theorem~\ref{theo:ec_read_cap_ub} is efficiently computable. We present the explicit semidefinite program in Appendix~\ref{app:UB_SDP}.

\begin{remark}
    We switch from the Umegaki relative entropy to the Belavkin--Staszewski relative entropy in~\eqref{eq:Umegaki_to_BS} to make use of the chain rule given in~\eqref{eq:ec_chain_rule_ing}. The Umegaki relative entropy also has a chain rule~\cite{FFRS20}, but it involves the regularized Umegaki relative entropy of channels, which is not known to be efficiently computable in general. However, for some special classes of channels, e.g.~the class of jointly classical-quantum channels, the regularization can be dropped. In this case, one can obtain an upper bound on the energy-constrained reading capacity in terms of the Umegaki relative entropy itself, which can be significantly tighter than the bound presented in Theorem~\ref{theo:ec_read_cap_ub}. We discuss the case of classical-quantum channels later in~\ref{sec:cq_channels}.
\end{remark}

In the absence of energy constraints, the upper bound in Theorem~\ref{theo:ec_read_cap_ub} can be expressed in a much cleaner form, as the information radius of the set of channels induced by the Belavkin--Staszewski relative entropy. We state this formally in Corollary~\ref{cor:read_cap_ub} below.

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:read_cap_ub}
    The reading capacity of a set of channels $\mathcal{N}\coloneqq \left\{\mathcal{N}^x\right\}_{x\in \mathcal{X}}$ is bounded from above by the following:
    \begin{equation}
        R\!\left(\mathcal{N}\right) \le \inf_{\mathcal{S}\in \operatorname{CPTP}}\max_{x\in \mathcal{X}}\widehat{D}\!\left(\mathcal{N}^x\middle\Vert\mathcal{S}\right),
    \end{equation}
    where the infimum is over all quantum channels.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
    We can rewrite~\eqref{eq:BS_rel_ent_le_avg_BS} as follows:
    \begin{align}
        \widehat{D}\!\left(\sigma^{(m)}_{\hat{M}}\middle\Vert\tau_{\hat{M}}\right) &\le \sum_{i=0}^{n-1}\operatorname{Tr}\!\left[\left(\operatorname{Tr}_{R_{i+1}}\!\left[\sigma^{i,m}_{AR_{i+1}}\right]\right)^T\widehat{D}_{\operatorname{op}}\!\left(\Gamma^{x_{i+1}(m)}_{AB},\Gamma^{\mathcal{S}}_{AB}\right)\right] \\
        &\le \sum_{i=0}^{n-1}\left\Vert\operatorname{Tr}_B\!\left[\widehat{D}_{\operatorname{op}}\!\left(\Gamma^{x_{i+1}(m)}_{AB},\Gamma^{\mathcal{S}}_{AB}\right)\right]\right\Vert_{\infty}\\
        &= \sum_{i=0}^{n-1}\widehat{D}\!\left(\mathcal{N}^{x_{i+1}}_{A\to B}\middle\Vert\mathcal{S}_{A\to B}\right)\\
        &\le \max_{x\in \mathcal{X}}n\widehat{D}\!\left(\mathcal{N}^x_{A\to B}\middle\Vert\mathcal{S}_{A\to B}\right),
    \end{align}
    where the second inequality follows from the fact that $\left(\operatorname{Tr}_{R_{i+1}}\!\left[\sigma^{i,m}_{AR_{i+1}}\right]\right)^T$ is a unit-trace positive semidefinite operator and the equality follows from~\eqref{eq:BS_ch_closed_form}. We know from~\eqref{eq:n_mess_umegaki_ub}, combined with~\eqref{eq:BS_ge_Umegaki}, that
    \begin{equation}
    \max_{m\in \mathcal{M}_n}\frac{1}{n(1-\varepsilon)}\!\left(\widehat{D}\!\left(\sigma^{(m)}_{\hat{M}}\middle\Vert\tau_{\hat{M}}\right) + h_2(\varepsilon)\right)   
    \end{equation}
    is an upper bound on the reading rate for a protocol that generates the state $\sigma^{(m)}_{\hat{M}}$ corresponding to the message $m\in \mathcal{M}_n$ and any worst-case error tolerance $\varepsilon \in [0,1)$. Therefore, for every reading protocol $\mathcal{P}^{(n)}$, number of channel uses $n \in \mathbb{N}$, and worst-case error-tolerance $\varepsilon \in [0,1)$, the reading rate from the set of channels $\mathcal{N}$ is bounded from above by the following:
    \begin{equation}
        \frac{\log_2|\mathcal{M}_n|}{n} \le \max_{x\in \mathcal{X}}\frac{1}{1-\varepsilon}\left(\widehat{D}\!\left(\mathcal{N}^x_{A\to B}\middle\Vert\mathcal{S}_{A\to B}\right)+\frac{h_2(\varepsilon)}{n}\right)
    \end{equation}
    for every channel $\mathcal{S}_{A\to B}$. The statement of the corollary then immediately follows from the definition of reading capacity.
\end{proof}

\medskip

\subsection{Lower bound on the energy-constrained reading capacity}

Now let us turn our attention to bounding the energy-constrained reading capacity of a set of channels from below. The authors of~\cite{DW19} showed that 
\begin{equation}\label{eq:DW_lb}
    R_{H,E}\!\left(\left\{\mathcal{N}^x_{A\to B}\right\}_{x\in \mathcal{X}}\right) \ge \sup_{p(x)}\sup_{\substack{\psi_{RA},\\ \operatorname{Tr}[H_A\psi_A] \le E}}I(X;RB)_{\omega},
\end{equation}
where $I(X:RB)_{\omega} \coloneqq S(\omega_X) + S(\omega_{RB}) - S(\omega_{XRB})$ is the mutual information of the state, $S(\rho) \coloneqq -\operatorname{Tr}[\rho\log_2\rho]$ is the von Neumann entropy, and
\begin{equation}
    \omega_{XRB} \coloneqq \sum_{x\in \mathcal{X}}p(x)|x\rangle\!\langle x|_X\otimes \mathcal{N}^x_{A\to B}\!\left(\psi_{RA}\right).
\end{equation}
The supremum in~\eqref{eq:DW_lb} is over every probability distribution $p(x)$ and every pure state $\psi_{RA}$ with energy less than or equal to $E$. This rate is achieved by a non-adaptive strategy, where each memory cell is probed by the same state $\psi_{RA}$.

While the right-hand side  of~\eqref{eq:DW_lb} is not efficiently computable, the inequality in~\eqref{eq:DW_lb} can be used to obtain a weaker but efficiently computable lower bound on the energy-constrained reading capacity. One can simply pick a probability distribution $p(x)$ and an input state $\psi_{RA}$, and the resulting mutual information $I(X;RB)_{\omega}$ is a lower bound on the energy-constrained reading capacity of the set of channels $\left\{\mathcal{N}^x_{A\to B}\right\}_{x\in \mathcal{X}}$.

Here, we simply rewrite the bound in terms of the Umegaki relative entropy of states. The Umegaki relative entropy of states can be approximated by a semidefinite program, as shown in~\cite{KS26}, which in turn allows us to obtain a bilinear programming lower bound on the energy-constrained reading capacity of a set of channels. The bilinear program is not efficiently computable, but similar to the lower bound from~\cite{DW19}, any feasible point of the bilinear program yields a valid lower bound on the energy-constrained reading capacity. The advantage of rewriting the bound in this way is that one can use algorithms like the see-saw algorithm to estimate a reasonable, sometimes tight, approximation of the value of the bilinear program, excluding the need to pick a ``good'' $p(x)$ and $\psi_{RA}$ in~\eqref{eq:DW_lb}. 

Let us first present the lower bound on the energy-constrained reading capacity as an optimization involving the Umegaki relative entropy of states.

\begin{proposition}\label{theo:read_cap_lb}
    The reading capacity of a set of channels $\mathcal{N}\coloneqq \left\{\mathcal{N}^x_{A\to B}\right\}_{x\in \mathcal{X}}$ is bounded from below by the following:
    \begin{equation}\label{eq:read_cap_lb}
        R_{H,E}\!\left(\mathcal{N}\right) \ge \sup_{\left\{p(x)\right\}_{x\in \mathcal{X}}}\sup_{\substack{\psi_{RA},\\ \operatorname{Tr}[H_A\psi_A] \le E}} \sum_{x\in \mathcal{X}}p(x)D\!\left(\mathcal{N}^x_{A\to B}\!\left(\psi_{RA}\right)\middle\Vert\overline{\mathcal{N}}_{A\to B}\!\left(\psi_{RA}\right)\right),
    \end{equation}
    where $\overline{\mathcal{N}}_{A\to B} \coloneqq \sum_{x\in \mathcal{X}}p(x)\mathcal{N}^x_{A\to B}$ the maximization is over all probability distributions over the set $\mathcal{X}$ and all pure states $\psi_{RA}$ satisfying $|R| = |A|$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
    Consider a non-adaptive reading protocol for a set of channels $\mathcal{N}\coloneqq \left\{\mathcal{N}^x\right\}_{x\in \mathcal{X}}$, where the reader uses a pure tensor-product probe state $\psi^{\otimes n}_{RA}$ to read the message $m$ encoded as $\bigotimes_{i=1}^n\mathcal{N}^{x_i(m)}_{A\to B}$, followed by a joint measurement. The probe state satisfies the energy constraint $\operatorname{Tr}\!\left[H_A\psi_A\right]\le E$. In principle, this is a protocol for transmitting classical information to a receiver using $n$ signal states from the set $\left\{\mathcal{N}^{x}_{A\to B}\!\left(\psi_{RA}\right)\right\}_x$. It has been shown in~\cite{SW97,Hol98} that the maximum rate at which classical information can be transmitted using a set of signal states $\left\{\rho^x\right\}_{x\in \mathcal{X}}$ reliably, that is the error vanishes with asymptotically large number of signals, with a priori probability distribution $\left\{p(x)\right\}_{x\in \mathcal{X}}$, is equal to the following:
    \begin{equation}
        C\!\left(\left\{p(x)\right\}_{x\in \mathcal{X}}, \left\{\rho^x\right\}_{x\in \mathcal{X}}\right) = S\!\left(\sum_{x\in \mathcal{X}}p(x)\rho^x\right) - \sum_{x\in \mathcal{X}}p(x)S\!\left(\rho^x\right).
    \end{equation}
    Note that the above expression can be written as follows~\cite[Proposition~7.14]{KW20}:
    \begin{equation}
        C\!\left(\left\{p(x)\right\}_{x\in \mathcal{X}}, \left\{\rho^x\right\}_{x\in \mathcal{X}}\right) = \sum_{x\in \mathcal{X}}p(x)D\!\left(\rho^x\Vert\overline{\rho}\right),
    \end{equation}
    where $\overline{\rho}\coloneqq \sum_{x\in \mathcal{X}}p(x)\rho^x$ and $D(\cdot\Vert\cdot)$ is the Umegaki relative entropy. It is then straightforward to see that if Alice encodes a message using the set of $\mathcal{N}$ with \emph{a priori} probability distribution $\left\{p(x)\right\}_{x\in \mathcal{X}}$, then the reading capacity of $\mathcal{N}$ is bounded from below by the following:
    \begin{equation}
        R_{H,E}\!\left(\mathcal{N}\right) \ge C\!\left(\left\{p(x)\right\}_{x\in \mathcal{X}}, \left\{\mathcal{N}^x_{A\to B}\!\left(\psi_{RA}\right)\right\}_{x\in \mathcal{X}}\right)
    \end{equation}
    Since the above inequality holds for every probability distribution $\left\{p(x)\right\}_{x\in \mathcal{X}}$ and every pure state $\psi_{RA}$ that satisfies $\operatorname{Tr}\!\left[H_A\psi_A\right]\le E$, we can conclude that
    \begin{align}
        R_{H,E}\!\left(\mathcal{N}\right) &\ge \sup_{\left\{p(x)\right\}_{x\in \mathcal{X}}}\sup_{\substack{\psi_{RA},\\ \operatorname{Tr}[H_A\psi_A]\le E}}C\!\left(\left\{p(x)\right\}_{x\in \mathcal{X}}, \left\{\mathcal{N}^x_{A\to B}\!\left(\psi_{RA}\right)\right\}_{x\in \mathcal{X}}\right)\\
        &= \sup_{\left\{p(x)\right\}_{x\in \mathcal{X}}}\sup_{\substack{\psi_{RA},\\ \operatorname{Tr}[H_A\psi_A]\le E}}\sum_{x\in \mathcal{X}}p(x)D\!\left(\mathcal{N}^x_{A\to B}(\psi_{RA})\middle\Vert\sum_{x\in \mathcal{X}}p(x)\mathcal{N}^x_{A\to B}(\psi_{RA})\right)\\
        &= \sup_{\left\{p(x)\right\}_{x\in \mathcal{X}}}\sup_{\substack{\psi_{RA},\\ \operatorname{Tr}[H_A\psi_A]\le E}}\sum_{x\in \mathcal{X}}p(x)D\!\left(\mathcal{N}^x_{A\to B}(\psi_{RA})\middle\Vert\overline{\mathcal{N}}_{A\to B}(\psi_{RA})\right),\label{eq:read_cap_lb_avg_ch}
    \end{align}
    where $\overline{\mathcal{N}}_{A\to B} \coloneqq \sum_{x\in \mathcal{X}}p(x)\mathcal{N}^x_{A\to B}$ and the supremum is over every probability distribution $\left\{p(x)\right\}_{x\in \mathcal{X}}$ and pure state $\psi_{RA}$ satisfying $\operatorname{Tr}\!\left[H_A\psi_A\right]\le E$. Since the Umegaki relative entropy is invariant under isometries and every purification of $\psi_A$ is related to the canonical purification via an isometry on the reference system, we can fix $|R| = |A|$, arriving at the statement of the proposition.
    \end{proof}

    \medskip

    Now we will relax the lower bound in Proposition~\ref{theo:read_cap_lb} to a bilinear program. First, consider the following integral representation of the Umegaki relative entropy discovered in~\cite{Jen24}:
    \begin{equation}\label{eq:rel_ent_int_rep}
        D(\rho\Vert\sigma) = \frac{1}{\ln 2}\left[\int_{\mu}^{\lambda}\frac{ds}{s}\operatorname{Tr}_{+}\!\left[\sigma s - \rho\right] + \ln \lambda + 1 - \lambda\right],
     \end{equation}
     which holds for every pair of states $\rho$ and $\sigma$ if $\mu \sigma \le \rho \le \lambda \sigma$. In~\eqref{eq:rel_ent_int_rep}, $\operatorname{Tr}_{+}[\tau]$ refers to the sum of the positive eigenvalues of the operator $\tau$. The authors of~\cite{KS26}  devised a hierarchy of semidefinite programs that estimate the Umegaki relative entropy using a discretized version of~\eqref{eq:rel_ent_int_rep} and the fact that $\operatorname{Tr}_+[\tau] = \sup_{0\le P\le I}\operatorname{Tr}[P\tau]$. Their results were extended to obtain semidefinite programming hierarchies for the Umegaki relative entropy of channels in~\cite{KW26}. Using their approach, one can obtain a bilinear programming lower bound on the energy-constrained reading capacity of a finite set of channels. We report this program in Appendix~\ref{app:LB_BP}. 




