\begin{abstract}
Deep thermalization indicates the emergence of universal ensembles of pure states in a subsystem after measuring its environment. We investigate this phenomenon in a free-fermion chain using local measurements that preserve Gaussianity and break particle-number conservation. Although the measurement outcomes provide partial information about local conserved charges within a finite subsystem, we argue that this information becomes negligible in the thermodynamic limit. This effective non-revelation underlies the emergence of a fermionic Gaussian Scrooge ensemble, determined by the subsystem's generalized Gibbs ensemble. We construct this universal ensemble directly at the subsystem level and provide numerical evidence that it describes the late-time projected ensemble at finite temperature. Our results extend the description of thermalization in non-interacting integrable systems beyond the reduced density matrix to universal statistics of conditional pure states.
\end{abstract}

\tableofcontents

\section{Introduction}

Understanding how equilibrium emerges from unitary quantum dynamics is a central problem in many-body physics. Conventional thermalization concerns the relaxation of local observables and is described by the reduced density matrix of a subsystem much smaller than its surrounding bath. In generic systems, the stationary states are locally described by Gibbs ensembles~\cite{d-91,s-94,s-99,rdo-08,polkov-11,dalessio-16,d-18}. In integrable systems, an extensive set of additional conserved quantities constrains relaxation, leading instead to a generalized Gibbs ensemble (GGE)~\cite{rigol-07,cef-11,ef-13,ilievski-15,ilievski-16,vr-16,pvc-16,piroli-17,sbkc-26}. These descriptions determine local expectation values, but do not address the additional information obtained by measuring the surrounding system and recording the outcome, which is now a standard feature of real-world quantum simulators. To take into account this additional information it is useful to consider the \emph{projected ensemble}: an ensemble of pure states conditioned on measurements of the partial state, weighted by their Born probability. The approach of this ensemble to universal distributions is known as the \emph{deep thermalisation}.

A recent surge of interest on this phenomenon has been triggered by experimental observations in a Rydberg-atom-array quantum simulator~\cite{choi-23}, together with theoretical studies~\cite{cotler-23,hc-22}. Subsequent experiments have explored universal quantum-state statistics in several platforms~\cite{shaw-25,yan-26,liu-26}. Numerous theoretical studies have characterized deep thermalization in quantum circuits~\cite{hc-22,cl-22,ih-22,ih-23,msr-26}, Hamiltonian systems~\cite{cotler-23,mark-24,sh-25}, random tensor networks~\cite{lami-26}, Gaussian bosonic~\cite{lhh-24} and fermionic systems~\cite{lpdd-23,bbm-25}, as well as extensions to mixed-state ensembles~\cite{yhk-25,mm-25,sr-26}, and instances in which it fails~\cite{bdp-23,lih-26,feng-26}. Beyond its fundamental significance, deep thermalization provides a source of quantum randomness for applications in quantum information, including benchmarking~\cite{choi-23,mark-23}, state learning~\cite{tran-23}, and quantum cryptography~\cite{chakraborty-25,cui-25} (see Ref.~\cite{mark-26} for a recent review).

A particularly interesting aspect of deep thermalization is the interplay between conservation laws and measurements~\cite{vb-24,chang-25}. Conserved quantities constrain the stationary reduced density matrix, while measurements of the environment can reveal additional information about the charges in the subsystem. In chaotic systems, measurements that reveal no additional charge information are expected to produce a Scrooge ensemble~\cite{mark-24,chang-25}, a Haar distribution distorted by the presence of the conservation laws~\cite{jrw-94,goldstein-06,goldstein-16}. Measurements that reveal charge information can instead lead to mixtures of Scrooge ensembles~\cite{chang-25}. Understanding which information survives in the thermodynamic limit is therefore essential for identifying the universal projected ensemble.

Integrable systems offer a particularly useful setting in which to address this question because of their many conservation laws, while free-fermion models permit the study of large systems through correlation matrix methods whose computational cost is only polynomial in the total system size. Previous work on free fermions introduced the \emph{deep generalized Gibbs ensemble} as an ansatz for the projected ensemble. The latter is constructed by measuring a representative global state with the appropriate conserved-charge densities~\cite{lpdd-23}. This construction, however, requires knowledge of the full state. Related work rigorously established convergence to a uniform ensemble of fermionic Gaussian states in random matchgate circuits in the absence of conserved charges~\cite{bbm-25}, while Gaussian Scrooge distributions were identified in bosonic continuous-variable systems~\cite{lhh-24}. These results motivate a description of the universal finite-temperature projected ensemble of Gaussian fermions directly on the subsystem.

In this work, we address this problem by investigating a Gaussian integrable Hamiltonian with a large number of conserved charges that are only partially accessible or revealed by the bath measurements. We examine how the information revealed about local conserved charges affects a fixed subsystem as the bath grows. Our analysis shows that the partial information about conserved charges becomes irrelevant in the thermodynamic limit, which motivates a universal description in terms of a fermionic Gaussian Scrooge ensemble constructed directly from the reduced $\rm{GGE}$.

The manuscript is organized as follows. In Sec.~\ref{sec:DT} we briefly review the concept of deep thermalization and discuss the state of the art. In Sec.~\ref{sec:model} we introduce the model and the measurement protocol analyzed in the rest of the manuscript. In Sec.~\ref{sec:inftemp} we first consider the simpler case of quenches leading to a locally infinite-temperature state, which allows us to highlight the asymptotic non-revelation of the conserved charges. In Sec.~\ref{sec:finittemp}, we generalize our predictions to finite-temperature quenches by introducing the fermionic Gaussian Scrooge ensemble. Finally, we present our conclusions in Sec.~\ref{sec:concl}. Further technical details and derivations are provided in the appendices.

