\section{Deep Thermalization}
\label{sec:DT}
In this section, we introduce the notion of projected ensemble and the idea underlying deep thermalization. Consider a pure state $\ket{\Psi}$ defined on a bipartite system $A\cup B$, where $A$ and $B$ respectively contain $L_A$ and $L_B$  qubits. In the following we identify $A$ as our subsystem of interest and $B$ with the bath and according consider $L_B \gg L_A$. Suppose now to projectively measure the bath $B$ in such a way as to fully determine the state in $B$, i.e.\ with a rank-1 projection. Without loss of generality, we can encode the outcome of the measurement in a binary string $\bm{z} \in \{0,1\}^{L_B}$. To each outcome $\bm{z}$, occurring with Born probability $p(\bm{z}) = \bra{\Psi}(\mathbb{I}_A \otimes \ket{\bm{z}}\!\bra{\bm{z}})\ket{\Psi}$, we can associate the projected pure state $\ket{\psi(\bm{z})}_A =(\mathbb{I}_A \otimes \bra{\bm{z}}) \ket{\Psi} /\sqrt{p(\bm{z})}$ in $A$. The projected ensemble $\mathcal{E}_{\rm{PE}}$ is a distribution of pure states in $A$ defined as the collection of projected states $\ket{\psi(\bm{z})}_A$ each weighted by its Born probability $p(\bm{z})$
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:def_PE}
\mathcal{E}_{\text{PE}} = \{p(\bm{z}), \ket{\psi(\bm{z})}_A\}.
\end{equation}
The first moment of $\mathcal{E}_{\rm{PE}}$ coincides with the partial trace over the bath, i.e.
\begin{equation}
 \rho_{\mathcal{E}_{\rm{PE}}}^{(1)}= \mathbb{E}_{\ket{\psi} \sim \mathcal{E}_{\rm{PE}}}[\ket{\psi}\!\bra{\psi}] = \text{Tr}_B[\ket{\Psi} \bra{\Psi}]=\rho_A,
\end{equation}
while higher moments of the projected ensemble
\begin{equation}  \rho_{\mathcal{E}_{\rm{PE}}}^{(k)} = \sum_{\bm{z}}p(\bm{z}) \left(\ket{\psi(\bm{z})}\!\bra{\psi(\bm{z})}\right)^{\otimes k},
\end{equation}
encode the statistics of conditional expectation values $\langle \mathcal{O}_{A}\rangle_{\bm{z}}$ of local operators in $A$ through
\begin{equation}
    \mathbb{E}_{\ket{\psi} \sim \mathcal{E}_{\rm{PE}}} \left[ \langle \mathcal{O}_{A}\rangle^k_{\bm{z}}\right] = \text{Tr}\left[\rho_{\mathcal{E}_{\rm{PE}}}^{(k)}\mathcal{O}_A^{\otimes k}\right].
\end{equation}

In isolated many-body systems, such as the one considered in this work, conventional thermalization is formulated in terms of the emergence of a local equilibrium state $\rho_{\mathrm{eq},A}$ so that 
\begin{equation}
  \lim_{t\to\infty}\lim_{L_{B}\to\infty} \bra{\Psi(t)}\mathcal{O}_A \ket{\Psi(t)} 
  =\text{Tr}_A[\rho_{\mathrm{eq},A} \mathcal{O}_A],
\end{equation}
which can be equivalently recast as a constraint on the first moment of the projected ensemble 
\begin{equation}
  \lim_{t\to\infty}\lim_{L_B\to\infty}\rho^{(1)}_{\mathcal{E}_{\rm{PE}}} 
  = \rho_{\mathrm{eq},A}.
\end{equation}
Deep thermalization extends this notion by asking whether the full hierarchy of higher moments, and therefore the full distribution of conditional pure states, also approach a universal form.

In the absence of conservation laws, the expected universal ensemble is the Haar ensemble of pure states~\cite{cotler-23,hc-22,ih-23,cl-22,ih-22}. Instead, in the presence of conservation laws, the local equilibrium state is generally not maximally mixed but instead retains information about the conserved quantities. Moreover, unlike in the conventional thermalization, the limiting projected ensemble is constrained not only by the conservation laws, but also by the measurement basis in the bath $B$ \cite{mark-24,vb-24,chang-25}. The effect of the measurement basis can be classified according to the amount of information that the measurement outcomes reveal about the conserved charge in $A$. Consider, for simplicity, a local $U(1)$ charge $Q = Q_A + Q_B$. Depending on the measurement basis, a certain outcome $\bm{z}$ can update the knowledge of the charge $Q_A$.  This motivates a general classification of measurement bases \cite{chang-25}, ranging from \textit{maximally charge revealing} basis, in which $\bm{z}$ uniquely determines the value of $Q_B$, to \textit{charge non-revealing} basis, in which conditioning on the measurement outcome leaves the subsystem charge distribution unchanged, see Sec.~\ref{subsec:nonrevel} for a more precise discussion.

For charge non-revealing measurement bases, the expected universal projected ensemble is the Scrooge ensemble~\cite{mark-24,chang-25} associated with the local equilibrium state. The Scrooge ensemble associated with a density matrix $\rho$ consists of the normalized states~\cite{jrw-94,goldstein-06,goldstein-16}
\begin{equation}
|\psi(\phi)\rangle
=\frac{\sqrt{\rho}\,|\phi\rangle}
{\sqrt{\langle\phi|\rho|\phi\rangle}},
\end{equation}
where $|\phi\rangle$ is sampled from the Haar measure with probability density $D\langle\phi|\rho|\phi\rangle$, and $D$ is the Hilbert-space dimension. This ensemble has average density matrix $\rho$ and reduces to the Haar ensemble when $\rho=\mathbb I/D$.
In particular, this prediction has been supported numerically for nonintegrable, energy-conserving many-body systems where the bath is measured in an energy-non-revealing basis~\cite{cotler-23,mark-24}. The underlying principle behind the emergence of Scrooge ensembles is the minimization of the \textit{accessible information}~\cite{holevo-98} or, equivalently, maximization of ensemble entropy over the accessible Hilbert space~\cite{mark-24}.

More generally, bath measurements can partially reveal the conserved charge. In the $U(1)$ setting described above, outcomes can be grouped into equivalence classes according to the conditional charge distribution $p(Q_A|\bm{z})$, defined using an appropriate ensemble of representative global states~\cite{chang-25}. Each class can then be associated with a Scrooge ensemble, giving rise to a mixture known as the generalized Scrooge ensemble~\cite{mark-24,chang-25}. 
The latter case is of particular relevance for Hamiltonian dynamics, in which, typically, energy does not admit a local fully revealing basis. For a generic measurement basis, explicitly specifying the mixture can require an amount of data that grows exponentially with bath size~\cite{mark-24}, raising questions on the degree of universality of the Scrooge ensemble. Understanding the interplay between the measurement basis and the conservation of generic finite-range local charges is thus crucial to determine to what extent the projected ensemble deeply thermalises to a truly universal ensemble.

\section{Model and measurement protocol}
\label{sec:model}

We consider a one-dimensional chain of spinless fermions on an even number $L$ of sites. The system is prepared in low-entangled, number-conserving, pure, Gaussian state $\ket{\Psi_0}$ and evolves in time with a unitary dynamics described by the quadratic Hamiltonian 
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:Hxx}
H_{\rm{XX}}= - \sum_{j=1}^L( c^{\dagger}_{j+1}c_j + c^{\dagger}_j c_{j+1}),
\end{equation}
where we assume periodic boundary conditions $c_{L+1} = c_1$. We consider a bipartition of the system into a subsystem $A$ corresponding to sites $j = 1,\cdots, L_A$ with $L_A$ even and the bath $B$ with the remaining $L_B = L-L_A$ sites. The model in Eq.~\eqref{eq:Hxx} is integrable with an extensive set of local conserved charges~\cite{fagotti-14,ef-16}, therefore, in the limit of $L_B \to \infty$ and $t \to \infty$ the stationary state in $A$ is described by the reduced GGE\footnote{Note that, since the initial state has fixed particle number, we do not need to consider the extra non-abelian charges in Eq.~\eqref{eq:xxcharges} and can describe the GGE solely in terms of occupation numbers.} 
\begin{equation}
    \rho_{\mathrm{GGE},A} = \frac{1}{Z} \text{Tr}_B\left[e^{- \sum_k \lambda(k)\, n_k} \right],
\end{equation}
with 
\begin{equation}
n_k = c^{\dagger}_k c_k, 
\quad\text{and}\quad
c_k = \frac{1}{\sqrt{L}} \sum_j e^{-ikj} c_j. 
\end{equation}
The Lagrange multipliers $\lambda(k) = \log((1-n(k))/{n(k)})$ are related to the momentum density occupation function $n(k) = \text{Tr}[\rho_0 n_k]$ of the initial state $\rho_0 = \ket{\Psi_0}\!\bra{\Psi_0}$. At any time $t$ of the quantum quench the state $\ket{\Psi(t)} = e^{- i H_{\rm{XX}}t} \ket{\Psi_0}$ is Gaussian, therefore all physical observables are encoded in the two-point correlation matrix $C_{ij} = \bra{\Psi} c^{\dagger}_i c_j \ket{\Psi}$. 

In the following, we will conveniently describe the system in terms of the $2 L$ Majorana fermions
\begin{equation}
\gamma_{j,1} = c_j + c^{\dagger}_j, \qquad  \gamma_{j,2} = i(c_j - c^{\dagger}_j),
\end{equation}
which satisfy the algebra $\{\gamma_{j,\alpha},\gamma_{k,\beta}  \} = 2 \delta_{jk} \delta_{\alpha \beta}$. Introducing the composite index $a = (j,\alpha)$, the Majorana correlation matrix $\Gamma$ of a state $\ket{\Psi}$ is defined as the real anti-symmetric matrix 
\begin{equation}
\Gamma_{ab} =i( \bra{\Psi} \gamma_{a} \gamma_b \ket{\Psi}- \delta_{ab}).
\end{equation}

\noindent \textbf{Measurement protocol.} Our goal is to projectively measure the bath $B$ such that, after the measurements, the projected state in $A$ is pure, Gaussian and, importantly, such that it breaks particle-number conservation, with the objective of having a particle number non-revealing measurement basis. 

We can achieve the required properties in the following way. We divide $B$ into $ (L-L_A)/2$ two-site cells and measure in each cell the following two Hermitian operators $M_1$ and $M_2$ (see Fig.~\ref{fig:sketch} for a sketch) acting on sites $2j-1,2j$
\begin{equation}\label{eq:defM12}
  \begin{aligned}
    M_{j,1} &\equiv  i \gamma_{2j-1,1} \gamma_{2j,1} 
    = i \left(c^{\dagger}_{2j-1} c_{2j} + c^{\dagger}_{2j-1} c^{\dagger}_{2j} - \mathrm{h.c.}\right),\\
    M_{j,2} &\equiv  i \gamma_{2j-1,2} \gamma_{2j,2} 
    = i \left(c^{\dagger}_{2j-1} c_{2j} - c^{\dagger}_{2j-1} c^{\dagger}_{2j} - \mathrm{h.c.}\right).
  \end{aligned}
\end{equation}
These two operators commute $\left[M_{j,1},M_{k,2}\right] = 0$ and are not proportional to each other, which implies that measuring them sequentially provides a rank-1 projection on the two-site cell. Moreover, since $M_{j,1}^2 = M_{j,2}^2 = 1$, we can write the projectors associated with different measurement outcomes $z = \pm 1$ as 
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:Pialpha}
\Pi^{(\alpha)}_{z} = \frac{\mathbb{I} + z M_{\alpha}}{2} \qquad \alpha = 1,2, 
\end{equation}
which preserves Gaussianity, as can be shown by writing each projector as
\begin{equation}
\frac{\mathbb{I} + z M_{\alpha}}{2} 
  = \lim_{\mu \to \infty} \frac{2\ e^{\mu z M_{\alpha}}}{\text{Tr}[e^{\mu z M_{\alpha}}]},

\end{equation}
with $M_{\alpha}$ a quadratic operator by definition. Observe that, in spin language, the measurement operators in Eq.~\eqref{eq:defM12} correspond to $M_{j,1} = Y_{2j-1} X_{2j}$ and $M_{j,2} = - X_{2j-1} Y_{2j}$, therefore, apart from an $i$ phase to the $2j$-qubit, the projected states in the two-site cell are Bell basis states~\cite{nc-10}.

Given two outcomes $w = \pm 1$ and $v = \pm 1$, respectively for $M_1$ and $M_2$, $\Pi_{v}^{(2)}\Pi_{w}^{(1)}$ is a rank-1 projector on a two-qubit Hilbert space. Since 
\begin{equation}
[M_{1,2},n_{2j-1}+ n_{2j}] \neq 0,
\end{equation}
where we set 
\begin{equation}
n_j=c^\dag_j c^{\phantom\dag}_j,
\end{equation}
the projected state breaks particle-number conservation. Performing the measurements for each disjoint two-site cell in the bath $B$, we obtain a pure Gaussian state in $A$.  We denote by $\bm{w} = \{w_i\}_{i = 1,\cdots,L_B/2}$ the collection of outcomes of the projective measurement of $M_{1}$ in the two-site cells of $B$, while we denote by $\bm{v} = \{v_i\}_{i = 1,\cdots,L_B/2}$ the outcomes for $M_2$. The total collection of outcomes $\bm{z} = \{\bm{w},\bm{v}\}$ defines the projected pure state $\ket{\psi(\bm{z})}_A$ of $A$ belonging to the projected ensemble $\mathcal{E}_{\rm{PE}}$ (cf.\ Eq.~\eqref{eq:def_PE}). Since $\mathcal{E}_{\rm{PE}}$ is an ensemble of Gaussian states, we can fully characterize it through the statistical moments of the ensemble of projected correlation matrices $\Gamma(\bm{z})$, the $k-$th moment is
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:PEmom}
\Gamma^{(k)}_{\rm{PE}} = \sum_{\bm{z}} p(\bm{z}) \,\Gamma(\bm{z})^{\otimes k}.
\end{equation}
In App.~\ref{app:app_Gammaz}, we describe how to analytically determine the correlation matrix $\Gamma(\bm{z})$ of the post-measurement Gaussian state in $A$.\\

\noindent \textbf{General family of Gaussian measurement protocols.} We have described a specific choice of the measurement operators $M_{1,2}$ in Eq.~\eqref{eq:defM12}. More generally, it is possible to construct a continuous family of measurement protocols with the same properties. To accomplish this, we can think of applying, to each two-site cell that will be monitored, the same Gaussian unitary operation $U_O = \exp(-\bm{\gamma}^T A \bm{\gamma}/4)$, with $A^T = -A$ and $e^A = O \in SO(4)$. We then measure the Majorana bilinears in Eq.~\eqref{eq:defM12}. With this procedure, by choosing $O \in SO(4)$ we can generate the full family of Gaussianity-preserving measurement protocols with measured bilinears given by $M_1^{(O)} = U_O^{\dagger} M_1 U_O$ and $M_2^{(O)} = U_O^{\dagger} M_2 U_O$ with a $SO(2) \times SO(2)$ redundancy given by independent rotations of the two Majorana operators in $M_{1,2}$ and by exchanging $M_1 \leftrightarrow M_2$. In the following, we refer to this general family of measurement protocols to discuss which physically relevant consequences are independent of the specific choice of measurement operators.

\section{Infinite-temperature projected ensemble}
\label{sec:inftemp}

We begin our analysis by considering the case of an infinite temperature quench. Hence, we require that the initial state $\ket{\Psi_0}$ satisfies the property $\bra{\Psi_0} c^{\dagger}_k c^{\phantom\dag}_k\ket{\Psi_0} = 1/2$ for any momentum $k$, which guarantees that the subsystem $A$ locally thermalizes to  the maximally mixed state $\rho_{\mathrm{GGE},A} = \mathbb{I}/2^{L_A}$. According to the logic introduced in Sec.~\ref{sec:DT}, the first step to understand the properties of the limiting projected ensemble is the analysis of the conserved charges present in the system and, crucially, of the amount of information they reveal.

\subsection{Effective non-revelation of local conserved charges}
\label{subsec:nonrevel}

We begin our analysis of the effect of the measurement basis by considering the fermionic parity, which is necessarily conserved by the dynamics. We then analyze explicitly the effect on the particle number and the two-local conserved charges present in the model in Eq.~\eqref{eq:Hxx}. \\

\noindent \textbf{Fermionic parity.} A first important observation is 
\begin{equation}
M_1 M_2 = - Z_{2j-1} Z_{2j} = - P,
\end{equation}
where $P = (-1)^{n_{2j-1}+n_{2j}}$ is the fermionic parity. This means that, if we denote by $v$ and $w$ the outcome of $M_1$ and $M_2$, respectively, the fermionic parity on the measured sites is uniquely determined by the measurement outcome. We denote the fermionic parity of the bath associated with $\bm{z}$ by $\pi_{\bm{z}}$. Note that this property is not specific to the measurement protocol chosen, as any protocol in the continuous family of measurement operators introduced in Sec.~\ref{sec:model} satisfies $M_1^{(O)} M_2^{(O)} = -P$ since $[U_O,P] = 0$ for any $O\in SO(4)$. \\

\noindent \textbf{Particle number.} Consider now the particle number 
\begin{equation}
N = \sum_{j} c^{\dagger}_j c^{\phantom\dag}_j = N_A + N_B.
\end{equation} 
Let us assume that, for a certain measurement outcome $\bm{z}$, there are $L_B/2-b_{\bm{z}}$ occurrences of $v = w$  such that the fermionic parity is odd. On such bonds, the outcome exactly fixes the number of particles on the bond to be $1$. On the remaining $b_{\bm{z}}$ bonds, because of our specific choice of measurement operators in Eq.~\eqref{eq:defM12}, the particle number is equally likely to be $0$ or $2$ with independent probabilities on different measured bonds. Hence, the exact conditional probability distribution of the particle number in $B$, given by
\begin{equation} 
\label{eq:pnbz}
  p_{\text{out}}(N_B|\bm{z}) 
  = \frac{1}{2^{b_{\bm{z}}}} \binom{b_{\bm{z}}}{\frac{N_B - (L_B/2-b_{\bm{z}})}{2}},
\end{equation}
when $N_B$ has the same parity as $L_B/2-b_{\bm{z}}$ and zero otherwise.
In particular, the conditional mean is $\langle N_B\rangle_{\bm{z}} = L_B/2$, while the conditional variance 
\begin{equation} 
\text{Var}_{\bm{z}}(N_B) \equiv \bra{\bm{z}} N_B^2 \ket{\bm{z}}- \bra{\bm{z}} N_B \ket{\bm{z}}^2 = b_{\bm{z}}.
\end{equation} 
Assuming local relaxation to infinite temperature, Chebyshev's inequality implies that (see App.~\ref{app:variance} for the details), for typical Born trajectories, the conditional number variance $\text{Var}_{\bm{z}}(N_B)$ $\simeq L_B/4$ is extensive in the bath size.

The result in Eq.~\eqref{eq:pnbz} highlights one of the main properties of our measurement protocol. Even though we have constructed the measurement to explicitly break the particle number conservation law, the bath distribution of particle number depends explicitly on the outcome $\bm{z}$, which will affect the knowledge of the subsystem distribution. According to the classification of Ref.~\cite{chang-25} described in Sec.~\ref{sec:DT}, our measurement basis is partially-revealing with respect to the particle number. We now show how the same property remains true for the higher local conserved charges.\\

\noindent \textbf{Higher-range conserved charges.} The complete set of local conserved charges for the $\rm{XX}$ model is given by \cite{fagotti-14,ef-16}
\begin{equation}\label{eq:xxcharges}

  \begin{aligned}
    H_r &= \sum_{j} c^{\dagger}_j c_{j+r} + c^{\dagger}_{j+r} c_j, &
    \Delta_r &= \sum_j (-1)^j (c_j c_{j+r} - c^{\dagger}_j c^{\dagger}_{j+r}),\\
    J_r &= i \sum_j (c^{\dagger}_j c_{j+r} - c^{\dagger}_{j+r} c_j),&
    \tilde{\Delta}_r &= i  \sum_j (-1)^j (c_j c_{j+r} + c^{\dagger}_j c^{\dagger}_{j+r}),

  \end{aligned}
\end{equation}
for $r\geq 1$, together with the particle number $N = \sum_j c^{\dagger}_j c_j$. We have included the $U(1)$ breaking charges $\Delta_r, \tilde{\Delta}_r$ because of the symmetry-breaking nature of the measurements. From the definition in Eq.~\eqref{eq:xxcharges}, we can immediately observe the following relations
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:JMDM}
  J_1 = \sum_j \frac{M_{1,j}  + M_{2,j}}{2}\quad\text{and}\quad
\Delta_1 = \sum_j (-1)^j \frac{M_{1,j}  - M_{2,j}}{2}.
\end{equation}
Consider for example the $J_1$ charge. We split its contributions into 
\begin{equation}
    J_1 = J_A + J_{AB} + J_{B,e} + J_{B,o},
\end{equation}
with $J_A$ containing terms only supported in the subsystem $A$, $J_{AB}$ contains terms supported both on $A$ and $B$. The remaining terms have support only in the bath $B$, with $J_{B,e}$ containing only the terms which are measured (even bonds) and $J_{B,o}$ the terms in between the measured bonds. Analysing as before the mean and variance of the distribution $p_{\text{out}}(J_B|\bm{z})$ of the bath charge $J_B = J_{B,e} + J_{B,o}$ after a certain outcome $\bm{z}$ we obtain
\begin{equation}
    \bra{\bm{z}} J_{B,e} \ket{\bm{z}} = \sum_{j_e} \frac{v_{j_e} + w_{j_e}}{2},
\end{equation}
on the measured bonds, while
\begin{equation}
    \bra{\bm{z}} J_{B,o} \ket{\bm{z}} = 0,
\end{equation}
on the unmeasured ones. The conditional variance $\text{Var}_{\bm{z}}(J_B)$ is exactly given by 
\begin{equation}
    \text{Var}_{\bm{z}}(J_B) = \frac{L_B}{4} -\frac{1}{2},
\end{equation}
independently of the outcome $\bm{z}$. Similar result holds for the other partially revealed two-local charge $\Delta_1$. With the same approach, one can easily verify that the posterior distribution of charges with larger support is also $\bm{z}$ dependent. An important caveat in the previous analysis is the fact that the conserved charges in the $\rm{XX}$ model are not mutually commuting, and we cannot define a joint probability distribution for the charges. We therefore characterize the information revealed by the measurements through the conditional distribution of each linear combination of charges.

Any conserved charge is described by an antisymmetric matrix $X$ in the Majorana operator space $Q_X = \frac{i}{4} \bm{\gamma}^T X \bm{\gamma}$ such that $[h_{\rm{XX}},X] = 0$, where $h_{\rm{XX}}$ is the one-particle representation of the $\rm{XX}$ Hamiltonian. For a fixed subsystem $A$ with $L_A$ sites we can only consider charges with local terms supported on at most $L_A$ sites. Any linear superposition $X_{\bm{u}} = \sum_j u_j X_j$ defines a conserved charge $Q_{\bm{u}}$. The correlation matrix in the post-measurement state $\Gamma_{\bm{z}}$ splits as $\Gamma_{A,\bm{z}} \oplus \Gamma_{B,\bm{z}}$ and the bath correlation matrix is
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:GBz}
    \Gamma_{B,\bm{z}} = \sum_{\alpha=1}^{L_B} z_\alpha E_{\alpha}.
\end{equation}
In Eq.~\eqref{eq:GBz}, the sum is over the components of $z$, $z_\alpha = \pm 1$, and $E_{\alpha}$ is a $2 L_B\times 2L_B$ matrix where the only non-zero entries are $(E_{\alpha})_{2\alpha-1,2\alpha}=-(E_{\alpha})_{2\alpha,2\alpha-1}=1$. 

For a fixed outcome $\bm{z}$, the average and variance of the bath charge can be obtained using Wick's theorem 
\begin{equation} \label{eq:muzVarz}
  \begin{aligned}
    \langle Q^B_{\bm{u}}\rangle_{\bm{z}} &= 
    -\frac{1}{4} \text{Tr}[X_{\bm{u}}\,\Gamma_{B,\bm{z}}],\\
    \text{Var}_{\bm{z}}(Q^B_{\bm{u}}) &= 
    \frac{1}{16} \text{Tr}[[X_{\bm{u}},\Gamma_{B,\bm{z}}]^T [X_{\bm{u}},\Gamma_{B,\bm{z}}]] = \frac{1}{16} ||[X_{\bm{u}},\Gamma_{B,\bm{z}}] ||^2_F,
  \end{aligned}
\end{equation}
where $X_{\bm{u}}$ is considered as the restriction on $B$ of the global charge matrix.
The first consequence of Eq.~\eqref{eq:muzVarz} is the absence of perfectly revealed conserved charges, i.e.\ such that $\text{Var}_{\bm{z}}(Q^B_{\bm{u}}) = 0$ for some fixed $\bm{u}$ and for any trajectory $\bm{z}$. The necessary condition would be $[X_{\bm{u}},\Gamma_{B,\bm{z}}] = 0$, for any $\bm{z}$, which requires $[X_{\bm{u}},E_{\alpha}] = 0$ for any $\alpha$. The last condition can only be satisfied by $X = \sum_\alpha x_{\alpha} E_{\alpha}$. Our specific measurement protocol is supported only on even bonds of the chain, while we know that $X_{\bm{u}}$ acts on both even and odd bonds, hence we can conclude that if $[X_{\bm{u}},h_{\rm{XX}}] = 0$ and $[X_{\bm{u}},\Gamma_{B,\bm{z}}] = 0$ then, necessarily, $X = 0$. 

For a generic conserved charge $Q_{\bm{u}}$, the conditional bath-charge distribution depends on the measurement outcome $\bm z$, as can be seen, for example, through its mean or variance. The measurement basis is thus partially revealing for a family of local conserved charges whose number grows linearly with the subsystem size $L_A$. This poses a question on the degree of universality of the projected ensemble, since we should track the effect of each outcome $\bm{z}$ on the subsystem. To discuss this, we closely follow the framework outlined in Ref.~\cite{chang-25}. We first identify a generator global state ensemble $\mathcal{F}$, which describes the characteristic late-time global states generated by the considered dynamics. For the $\rm{XX}$ dynamics studied here, a natural choice is to model $\mathcal{F}$ as a uniform distribution over all microstates compatible with the macrostate specified by an occupation function $n(k)$ fixed by the initial state.
Let us consider the case of infinite-temperature, in which $n(k) = 1/2$ for all $k$. We can model, in this case, the generator ensemble $\mathcal{F}_{\beta = 0}$ in a simpler way as
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:Fbeta0}
    \mathcal{F}_{\beta = 0} = \left\{\frac{1}{\binom{L}{L/2}}, \,\, \ket{\bm{n}} : \sum_{k=1}^L n_k = L/2 \right\}.
\end{equation}
Note that $\mathcal{F}_{\beta = 0}$ also contains states that are not microstates compatible with $n(k) = 1/2$. One example is $n_k = 1$ for $k = 1,\cdots,L/2$ and $n_k = 0$ otherwise. The fraction of these atypical states in $\mathcal{F}_{\beta = 0}$ is, however, vanishing in the thermodynamic limit. The reason behind this choice is the fact that the $\mathcal{F}_{\beta = 0}$-averaged state acquires the simple form
\begin{equation} \label{eq:Favgstate}
  \overline{\rho} = 
  \mathbb{E}_{\ket{\psi}\sim\mathcal{F}_{\beta = 0}}[\ket{\psi}\!\bra{\psi}] 
  = \frac{\Pi_{N,L/2}}{\binom{L}{L/2}},
\end{equation}
where we denoted by $\Pi_{O,\lambda}$ the projector onto the eigenspace of $O$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\lambda$. The state in Eq.~\eqref{eq:Favgstate} can be decomposed into a sum of tensor products of projectors acting on $A$ and $B$ separately
\begin{equation} \label{eq:Favg}
  \overline{\rho} =\frac{1}{\binom{L}{L/2}} \sum_{n = 0}^{L_A} \Pi^{(A)}_{N_A,n} \otimes \Pi^{(B)}_{N_B,L/2-n}.
\end{equation}
Our goal is to understand the effect of an outcome $\bm{z}$ on average over the generator ensemble. The average state after the outcome $\ket{\bm{z}}$ is
\begin{equation}
  \rho_{\bm{z}} 
  = \text{Tr}_B\left[\,\overline{\rho} \,(\mathbb{I}_A \otimes \ket{\bm{z}}\!\bra{\bm{z}})
  \right]/p(\bm{z}),
\end{equation}
with $p(\bm{z}) = \text{Tr}\left[\,\overline{\rho}\,(\mathbb{I}_A \otimes \ket{\bm{z}}\!\bra{\bm{z}})\right]$ the associated Born probability. The conditional state $\rho_{\bm{z}}$ defines an equivalence relation between different outcomes, two outcomes $\bm{z}_1$ and $\bm{z}_2$ are equivalent $\bm{z}_1 \sim \bm{z}_2$ if $\rho_{\bm{z}_1} = \rho_{\bm{z}_2}$. Using the explicit form of $\overline{\rho}$ in Eq.~\eqref{eq:Favg}, we obtain
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:finalrhoz}
    \rho_{\bm{z}} = \frac{\sum_{n = 0}^{L_A} p_{\text{out}}(L/2-n|\bm{z}) \,\Pi^{(A)}_{N_A,n}}{\sum_{n = 0}^{L_A} p_{\text{out}}(L/2-n|\bm{z}) d^{(A)}_n},
\end{equation}
where $d^{(A)}_n = \binom{L_A}{n}$ is the dimension of the sector with particle number $n$ in $A$. 
We can make use of our knowledge of the post-measurement bath charge distribution $p_{\text{out}}(m|\bm{z}) = \bra{\bm{z}} \Pi^{(B)}_{N_B,m} \ket{\bm{z}}$ in the limit of large bath $L_B \to \infty$. From the distribution obtained in Eq.~\eqref{eq:pnbz}, we can assume $p_{\text{out}}(m|\bm{z})$ to approach a Gaussian form on the values compatible with the parity $\pi_{\bm{z}}$ fixed by $\bm{z}$
\begin{equation}
    p_{\text{out}}(m|\bm{z}) \simeq \frac{2}{\sqrt{2 \pi \sigma_{\bm{z}}^2}} \exp \left[-\frac{(m-\mu_{\bm{z}})^2}{2 \sigma_{\bm{z}}^2}\right],
\end{equation}
where $\mu_{\bm{z}} = L_B/2$ and, as we have discussed, for typical Born trajectories we have $\sigma_{\bm{z}}^2 = O(L_B)$ as $L_B \to \infty$. Since in Eq.~\eqref{eq:finalrhoz}, we are interested in $m = L/2-n$, we have $m-\mu_{\bm{z}} = L_A/2 -n$ which varies on a finite scale compared to the diverging bath size $L_B$. To compare the probabilities entering the conditional state~\eqref{eq:finalrhoz}, we therefore consider
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:ratiop}
    \frac{p_{\text{out}}(m + \delta |\bm{z})}{p_{\text{out}}(m |\bm{z})} \simeq \exp\left(-\frac{\delta^2}{2 \sigma_{\bm{z}}^2} - \frac{(m-\mu_{\bm{z}}) \delta}{\sigma_{\bm{z}}^2}\right),
\end{equation}
where $\delta = O(L_A)$ and even to respect the parity constraint. From Eq.~\eqref{eq:ratiop}, taking now the limit of large $L_B$ we obtain 
\begin{equation}
     \frac{p_{\text{out}}(m + \delta |\bm{z})}{p_{\text{out}}(m |\bm{z})} = 1 +O(L_A^2/L_B),
\end{equation}
plugging this result in Eq.~\eqref{eq:finalrhoz} we obtain the maximally mixed state in the subsystem with parity sector fixed by $\bm{z}$. Denoting the projector onto such space as $\Pi^{(A)}_{P_A,\pi_{\bm{z}}}$, we have
\begin{equation}
    \rho_{\bm{z}} = \frac{\Pi^{(A)}_{P_A,\pi_{\bm{z}}}}{2^{L_A-1}} + O\left(\frac{L_A^2}{L_B}\right),
\end{equation}  
which is, crucially, asymptotically independent of the outcome $\bm{z}$ within the fixed parity sector. Therefore, measurement outcomes are divided in only two equivalence classes, determined by the bath fermionic parity $\pi_{\bm{z}}$. To summarize, although most local charges are partially revealed by the measurement basis, in the thermodynamic limit, the information obtained from the measurements is completely washed out in $A$. Effectively, within this generator-ensemble description, all charges are maximally non-revealed to the subsystem as $L_B \to \infty$. As discussed in \cite{chang-25}, in this scenario, the pure states of $A$ in the projected ensemble are conjectured to be drawn from the uniform, maximally entropic manifold of states. In our case, this ensemble corresponds to the Gaussian Haar ensemble, defined as~\cite{bhk-21,bianchi-22,mcp-22}
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:GHEdef}
	\mathcal{E}_{\rm{GHE}} = \left\{ O \sim \text{Haar}(O(2 L_A)),\,\,\Gamma(O) = O J O^T\right\},
\end{equation}
in which $J = i \sigma_y \otimes \mathbb{I}_{L_A}$, the ensemble generates correlation matrices in the manifold $\Gamma \in O(2 L_A)/U(L_A)$~\cite{az-97}. This ensemble automatically takes into account the exact revelation of the fermionic parity discussed at the beginning of this section, since it is an equal mixture of the uniform ensemble on the two different parity components
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{E}_{\rm{GHE}} = p_{\rm{e}} \,\mathcal{E}_{\rm{GHEe}} +  p_{\rm{o}}\, \mathcal{E}_{\rm{GHEo}},
\end{equation}
with $p_{\rm{e}} = p_{\rm{o}} = 1/2$. The fixed parity ensemble $\mathcal{E}_{\rm{GHE}e}$ is obtained by taking ${O \sim \text{Haar}(SO(2L_A))}$, while $\mathcal{E}_{\rm{GHE}o}$ by multiplying each $O$ by some fixed reflection $R \in O(2L_A)$ with $\mathrm{det} R = -1$. The Gaussian Haar Ensemble $\mathcal{E}_{\rm{GHE}}$ and the fixed parity ensembles $\mathcal{E}_{\rm{GHE}e/o}$ produce exactly the same predictions when considering $\mathbb{E}[\Gamma^{\otimes k}]$ for $k<L_A$, while for $k\geq L_A$, the moments of the correlations matrix are generally different, making the difference in the two predictions observable, see App.~\ref{app:parityGHE} for a derivation. Moreover, as we show in App.~\ref{app:parityGHE}, the average entanglement entropy is unaffected by the presence of a single parity sector for all bipartitions. 

\subsection{Symmetry classification of the projected ensemble}
The Gaussian Haar ensemble $\mathcal{E}_{\rm{GHE}}$ in Eq.~\eqref{eq:GHEdef} uniformly samples the manifold of pure Gaussian states. The manifold of states explored by the projected ensemble, however, may be constrained by global symmetries preserved at the level of individual quantum trajectories. Here, we provide a general
classification of the trajectory symmetries and their constraints on the projected ensemble that can be realized by the unitary dynamics generated by $H_{\rm{XX}}$ combined with the measurement protocol in Eq.~\eqref{eq:Pialpha}.

In general, let $H$ be a quadratic Hamiltonian and $\{M_j\}_j$ be a set of mutually commuting measurement operators such that their measurement with outcome $\bm{z}$ provides a rank one projector on the bath $B$. We denote by $K_{\bm{z}} = \mathbb{I}_A \otimes \Pi_{\bm{z}}^{(B)}$ the Kraus operator associated with the non-unitary evolution due to the projection onto the measurement outcome. The evolution of an initial pure Gaussian state $\rho_0$ is
\begin{equation}
\check{\rho}_{\bm{z}} = K_{\bm{z}} e^{-i H t} \rho_0 e^{i H t} K^{\dagger}_{\bm{z}},
\end{equation}
here $\check{\rho}_{\bm{z}}$ is unnormalized and $p(\bm{z}) = \text{Tr}[\check{\rho}_{\bm{z}}]$ is the Born probability for the outcomes $\bm{z}$, while the normalized projected pure state in $A$ is $\rho_A(\bm{z}) = \text{Tr}_B[\check{\rho}_{\bm{z}}]/p(\bm{z})$. We say that the full system monitored dynamics has a symmetry if there exists a unitary (or antiunitary) operator $U$ such that, for any time $t$, we have 
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:traj_sym}
    U K_{\bm{z}} e^{- i H t} U^{-1}= K_{\bm{z}} e^{- i H t}, \qquad \text{ for any }\bm{z}.
\end{equation}
Equation \eqref{eq:traj_sym} implies, for $H$ and the measurement operator $M_j$,
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:sym}
    \begin{cases}
        U M_j U^{-1}= M_j\,\, \forall j, \qquad U H U^{-1} = H, \qquad &\text{if } U \text{ unitary},\\
        U M_j U^{-1}= M_j\,\, \forall j, \qquad U H U^{-1} = -H, \qquad &\text{if } U \text{antiunitary}.
    \end{cases}
\end{equation}
If we can find an operator $U$ satisfying these conditions and also the initial state is symmetric, i.e.\ $U \rho_0 U^{-1} = \rho_0$, the infinite temperature projected ensemble at late time is expected to be given, according to what we argued in the previous subsection, by the uniform measure over the manifold of $U-$invariant states.

Based on this logic, we now identify the Gaussian trajectory symmetries relevant to our dynamics and measurement protocol. Throughout this classification, we impose fermion-parity superselection and consider only transformations that act nontrivially within a fixed parity sector.
It is convenient to first analyze the condition on the measurement operators. This allows us to classify all of the stabilizers of $M_{j,\alpha}$ for $\alpha=1,2$ (see definition in Eq.~\eqref{eq:defM12}) and for a fixed $j$, which restricts us to the group $SO(4)$ of parity even (anti-)unitaries, acting on the Majorana fermions as $U_O \gamma_a U^{-1}_O = \sum_b O_{ab} \gamma_b$. We are labeling the Majorana on the physical sites $2j$ and $2j+1$ as $\gamma_a$ for $a = 1,\cdots,4$, such that $M_1 = i \gamma_{1} \gamma_3$ and $M_2 = i \gamma_2 \gamma_4$.

Requiring $U_O M_{\alpha} U^{-1}_O = M_{\alpha}$ further restricts the group of stabilizers into the subgroup $SO(2)_{13} \times SO(2)_{24}$ of independent rotations of the Majoranas present in each $M_{\alpha}$. It is now easy to explicitly verify that, if $U_O$ is unitary, a necessary (but not sufficient) condition for the Hamiltonian condition in Eq.~\eqref{eq:sym} being satisfied is the restriction of the rotation angles to either $0$ or $\pi$. This reduces the possibilities to a discrete subgroup $\mathbb{Z}_2$ of special orthogonal matrices such that $O_{a b} = \delta_{a b} \epsilon_a$, with $\epsilon_a = \pm 1$ and with $\epsilon_1 = \epsilon_3 = 1$ and $\epsilon_2 \epsilon_4 = 1$. Similarly, if $U_O$ is antiunitary, we only have a $\mathbb{Z}_2$ subgroup formed by $O_{a b} = \delta_{a b} \epsilon_a$ with $\epsilon_1 = - \epsilon_3 = -1$ and $\epsilon_2 \epsilon_4 = -1$.

Specialising to the case of the $\rm{XX}$ Hamiltonian, we find that, among the remaining valid operators, the only transformation satisfying \eqref{eq:traj_sym} is one of the two possible antiunitary transformations, we denote it by $\mathcal{T}$. Because of antiunitarity $\mathcal{T}i \mathcal{T}^{-1} = -i$, the action on the complex fermions operators is $\mathcal{T} c_j \mathcal{T}^{-1} = (-1)^{j+1} c_j$, while for Majorana operators
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:Tau_def}
\mathcal{T} \gamma_{j,1} \mathcal{T}^{-1} = (-1)^{j+1} \gamma_{j,1}, \qquad
\mathcal{T} \gamma_{j,2} \mathcal{T}^{-1} = (-1)^{j} \gamma_{j,2}.
\end{equation}
We have thus proven that there are only two possible candidates projected ensembles that can arise from our model. Then, according to the assumption of effective non-revelation of the conserved charges, if the initial state $\ket{\Psi_0}$ is not invariant under $\mathcal{T}$, i.e.\ $\mathcal{T}\ket{\Psi_0} \neq e^{i\varphi} \ket{\Psi_0}$ there is no symmetry constraint and the limiting projected ensemble explores the full manifold of Gaussian states uniformly, hence producing the ensemble $\mathcal{E}_{\rm{GHE}}$ in Eq.~\eqref{eq:GHEdef}.

If instead $\ket{\Psi_0}$ is invariant under $\mathcal{T}$, the manifold of explored states will be constrained such that each state in the ensemble remains $\mathcal{T}-$invariant. We denote the uniform distribution over such manifold as constrained Gaussian Haar Ensemble $\mathcal{E}_{\rm{cGHE}}$. After ordering the Majorana operators based on their $\mathcal{T}$ sign, we can write the Majorana correlation matrix as
\begin{equation}
    \Gamma = \begin{pmatrix}
        0 & R\\
        -R^T & 0
    \end{pmatrix},
\end{equation}
where $R \in O(L_A)$, sampling $R$ from the Haar measure in $O(L_A)$ defines the ensemble $\mathcal{E}_{\rm{cGHE}}$, see details in App.~\ref{app:cGHE}. In the remaining of this section we will consider both scenarios and verify numerically the emergence of these universal ensembles as late-time projected ensemble arising from Gaussian Hamiltonian dynamics.

\subsection{Numerical verifications}
In this subsection, we provide numerical evidence for the late-time convergence of the projected ensemble to our universal predictions. To do so, we numerically sample, according to the Born probability, the projected ensemble obtained from the state 
\begin{equation}
\ket{\Psi(t)} = e^{-i H_{\rm{XX}}t} \ket{\Psi_0}.
\end{equation}

To test the convergence of the projected ensemble to our universal predictions, we compute
\begin{equation}
  \Delta^{(k)} = \left \Vert \sum_{\bm{z}} p(\bm{z}) \Gamma^{\otimes k}(\bm{z}) 
  - \Gamma^{(k)}_{\mathcal{E}}\right \Vert_F,
\end{equation}
where $\left\Vert\cdot \right\Vert_F$ is the Frobenius norm, and $\mathcal{E}$ is the target universal ensemble. As a further check, following Ref.~\cite{lpdd-23}, we compute the projected ensemble average of the space-averaged von Neumann entanglement entropy of the subsystem $A$ defined as
\begin{equation} \label{eq:Savg}
\overline{S}_A(\bm{z}) = \frac{1}{L_A} \sum_{i = 1}^{L_A-1} S_{1}(\rho_{A_i} (\bm{z})),
\end{equation}
where $\rho_{A_i}(\bm{z}) = \text{Tr}_{\{i+1,\cdots,L_A\}}\left[ \ket{\psi(\bm{z})}\!\bra{\psi(\bm{z})}\right]$ and $S_1(\rho) = -\text{Tr}\left[\rho \log \rho\right]$ is the von Neumann entanglement entropy. We remark that the average over the projected ensemble $\overline{S}_A \equiv \mathbb{E}_{\bm{z} \sim \mathcal{E}_{\rm{PE}}} \left[\overline{S}_A(\bm{z})\right]$ depends on all the moments of $\mathcal{E}_{\rm{PE}}$, thus providing a non-trivial check on the full hierarchy of moments. 

We start our numerical analysis by considering, as initial state, the Néel state 
\begin{equation}
\ket{\rm{N}} = \left(\prod_{k=1}^{L/2} c^{\dagger}_{2k}\right) \ket{0},
\end{equation}
with $\ket{0}$ the vacuum of the complex fermions $c_j,c^{\dagger}_j$. We observe that $\mathcal{T} \ket{\rm{N}} = - \ket{\rm{N}}$, hence we predict the convergence of $\mathcal{E}_{\rm{PE}}$ to the constrained ensemble $\mathcal{E}_{\rm{cGHE}}$. The left panel of Fig.~\ref{fig:infT} numerically confirms our prediction.
The distances $\Delta^{(k)}$ for $k = 1,2,3,4$ go to zero exhibiting a power law behaviour as already observed for local density measurements in \cite{lpdd-23}. The space-averaged entanglement entropy is also in agreement with the prediction from the ensemble $\mathcal{E}_{\rm{cGHE}}$ we have proposed based on the analysis of the discrete symmetries constraining the explored manifold of projected states and on the assumption of the effective non-revelation of all conserved charges. 

As an initial state breaking the $\mathcal{T}-$ symmetry locally relaxing to an infinite temperature state we can choose the ground state of the critical staggered Su–Schrieffer–Heeger (SSH) chain 
\begin{equation}
H_{\rm{SSH}} = \sum_j (-1)^j c^{\dagger}_j c_{j+1} + h.c.,
\end{equation}
given by 
\begin{equation}
\ket{\Psi_{\rm{SSH}}} = \prod_{|k| < \pi/2} \frac{c^{\dagger}_k + i\, \text{sgn}(\sin k)\,c^{\dagger}_{k+\pi}}{\sqrt{2}} \ket{0}.
\end{equation}
Explicit computations show: (i) $\langle\Psi_{\rm{SSH}}|\hat n_k|\Psi_{\rm{SSH}}\rangle=1/2$ for every $k$, and (ii) $\mathcal{T} \ket{\Psi_{\rm{SSH}}} \neq e^{i \varphi} \ket{\Psi_{\rm{SSH}}}$.

In the right panel of Fig.~\ref{fig:infT} we verify the convergence of the late-time projected ensemble to the unconstrained Gaussian Haar random ensemble $\mathcal{E}_{\rm{GHE}}$ (see Eq.~\eqref{eq:GHEdef}) from this initial state.
In Appendix~\ref{app:Ctridiag} we show how to construct a family of states satisfying (i) and (ii).

