\section{Finite-temperature projected ensemble}
\label{sec:finittemp}
In this section, we consider the case of a quench locally relaxing to a finite temperature GGE. We use as initial state a dimer state
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:dimer}
 \ket{\Psi_0(\alpha)} = \prod_{j=1}^{L/2} \frac{c^{\dagger}_{2j-1} + \alpha c^{\dagger}_{2j}}{\sqrt{1 + |\alpha|^2}} \ket{0}.
\end{equation}
This state, time evolved with the Hamiltonian~\eqref{eq:Hxx}, locally thermalizes to a GGE $\rho_{\rm{GGE}}(\alpha)$ such that $\text{Tr}[\rho_{\rm{GGE}}(\alpha) n_k] = \bra{\Psi_0(\alpha)}n_k \ket{\Psi_0(\alpha)} \equiv n(k)$. In particular, for the dimer state in Eq.~\eqref{eq:dimer}, one has
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:nk_dimer}
n(k) = \frac{1}{2} + \text{Re}\left[\frac{e^{-ik} \alpha}{1+ |\alpha|^2}\right].
\end{equation}

A first attempt to characterize the finite-temperature projected ensemble for the same free-fermionic unitary dynamics studied in this work but with local density measurements was proposed in Ref.~\cite{lpdd-23}, where the authors define a representative state $\ket{\Phi}$ of the global system whose conserved charges densities coincide, in the thermodynamic limit, with those of the initial state $\ket{\Psi_0(\alpha)}$. The representative state $\ket{\Phi}$ in the bath is then projectively measured, with outcomes sampled from the corresponding Born probability. The resulting ensemble of pure states in $A$ obtained in this way was termed \textit{deep}-GGE and in the following will be denoted as $\mathcal{E}_{\rm{dGGE}}$. It was shown that $\mathcal{E}_{\rm{dGGE}}$ numerically reproduces the finite moments of the projected ensemble obtained from the unitary dynamics when approaching the limit $L_B \to \infty$ and, subsequently, $t\to \infty$. As an explicit example, a simple possible way to fix the representative state $\ket{\Phi}$ for the $\rm{XX}$ dynamics consists in choosing a single eigenstate of $H_{\rm{XX}}$ at half-filling 
\begin{equation}
\ket{\Phi} = c^{\dagger}_{k_1}\cdots c^{\dagger}_{k_{L/2}} \ket{0}.
\end{equation}
The occupied modes are chosen such that the density of occupied modes reproduces, in the limit $L\to\infty$, the density $n(k)$ of occupied modes of the initial dimer state (Eq.~\eqref{eq:nk_dimer}). In practice, one asks that in an interval $[k, k + \Delta k]$ there are $L/(2\pi) \, n(k) \Delta k$ occupied modes.

Although the deep-GGE construction numerically reproduces the limiting projected ensemble, it is formulated in terms of a \textit{global} representative state and requires the explicit evaluation of the Born probabilities associated with the bath measurements. In the following we solve these problems by introducing a Scrooge ensemble on the manifold of fermionic Gaussian states, defined only in the \emph{local} subsystem of interest, which we argue describes the limiting projected ensemble. Interestingly, a similar constrained analogue of the Scrooge ensemble was obtained in continuous variable systems~\cite{lhh-24}, here we argue for its emergence in a deterministic non-interacting fermionic quantum system.

\subsection{Fermionic Gaussian Scrooge Ensemble}

Let $\ket{\phi_{\Gamma}}$ be a pure Gaussian state with Majorana correlation matrix $\Gamma$, and let $\rho_{\rm{GGE}}$ be the Generalized Gibbs ensemble attained by the unitary dynamics in the limit $L_B \to \infty$ and $t\to \infty$. We then define the Gaussian Scrooge Ensemble as the following ensemble of pure states in subsystem $A$\begin{equation}
\label{eq:GScrooge}
    \mathcal{E}_{GS} = \left\{ D_A \bra{\phi_{\Gamma}} \rho_{\mathrm{GGE},A} \ket{\phi_{\Gamma}} d\mu_{\rm{H}}(\Gamma) , \,\,\,\ket{\psi_{\Gamma'}} = \frac{\sqrt{\rho_{\mathrm{GGE},A}} \ket{\phi_{\Gamma}}}{\sqrt{\bra{\phi_{\Gamma}} \rho_{\mathrm{GGE},A}\ket{\phi_{\Gamma}}}}\right\},
\end{equation}
where $d\mu_{\rm{H}}(\Gamma)$ denotes the invariant measure over the manifold $O(2L_A)/U(L_A)$ of Gaussian pure states and $D_A = 2^{L_{A}}$ is the dimension of the Hilbert space of $A$. It is easy to check that the first moment of the Gaussian Scrooge Ensemble coincides with the subsystem reduced $\rho_{\mathrm{GGE},A}$
\begin{equation}

\mathbb{E}_{\ket{\psi}\sim\mathcal{E}_{GS}}[ \ket{\psi}\!\bra{\psi}] = 
  D_A \sqrt{\rho_{\mathrm{GGE},A}} \left( \int d\mu_{\rm{H}}(\Gamma) 
  \ket{\phi_{\Gamma}}\!\bra{\phi_{\Gamma}}\right) \sqrt{\rho_{\mathrm{GGE},A}} 
  =\rho_{\mathrm{GGE},A},

\end{equation}
where we used the fact that $\int d\mu_{\rm{H}}(\Gamma)\ket{\phi_{\Gamma}}\!\bra{\phi_{\Gamma}} = \mathbb{I}/D_A$. The effect of the $\sqrt{\rho_{\mathrm{GGE},A}}$-distortion of the Gaussian Haar Ensemble can be encoded in the Majorana correlation matrix as the following non-linear transformation
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:gammadist}
    \Gamma' = \Gamma_{\mathrm{GGE},A} + S \Gamma (\mathbb{I}-\Gamma_{\mathrm{GGE},A} \Gamma)^{-1} S,
\end{equation}
where $ S = \sqrt{\mathbb{I} + \Gamma_{\mathrm{GGE},A}^2}$, see App.~\ref{app:rhodist} for the derivation. The reweighing factor of the Haar measure is explicitly given by
\begin{equation}
    \bra{\phi_{\Gamma}} \rho_{\mathrm{GGE},A}\ket{\phi_{\Gamma}} = 2^{-L_A} \sqrt{\det(\mathbb{I} -\Gamma_{\mathrm{GGE},A}\Gamma)}.
\end{equation}

To argue for the emergence of $\mathcal{E}_{GS}$ we should, as before, show that charges are not revealed by the measurements. In this case, however, we cannot use our previous argument based on the global generator ensemble. Indeed, a simple decomposition of $\mathcal{F}$-averaged state as the one in Eq.~\eqref{eq:Favg} is not available at finite temperature. Nevertheless, similar to the post-measurement particle number distribution in Eq.~\eqref{eq:pnbz}, in App.~\ref{app:variance} we show that generic conserved charges display an extensive variance for Born-typical trajectories. This suggests that, in the thermodynamic limit, the effective non-revelation persists. 

We verify this prediction numerically, by determining exactly the reduced correlation matrix $\Gamma_{\mathrm{GGE},A}$ for the initial state $\ket{\Psi_0(\alpha)}$ (Eq.~\eqref{eq:dimer}) and then sampling numerically the fermionic Gaussian Scrooge ensemble in Eq.~\eqref{eq:GScrooge}.
Note that the initial dimer state $\ket{\Psi_0(\alpha)}$ is not $\mathcal{T}$-invariant for $\mathfrak{Re}\, \alpha \neq 0$, so no constraint is applied to the manifold of Gaussian states explored by the Haar measure $d\mu_{\rm{H}}(\Gamma)$. In Fig.~\ref{fig:finT}, we compare the finite-time projected ensemble with the predictions of the Gaussian Scrooge ensemble and the single-eigenstate approach of Ref.~\cite{lpdd-23}. The results agree within statistical uncertainties, supporting the Gaussian Scrooge ensemble as a description of the universal limiting projected ensemble formulated directly at the subsystem level.

