\section{Conclusions}
\label{sec:concl}

In this manuscript, we have investigated deep thermalization in the $\rm{XX}$ chain under local projective measurements preserving Gaussianity while breaking particle number conservation. We have provided numerical evidence that the limiting projected ensemble at finite temperature of Gaussian Hamiltonian dynamics is given by a fermionic Gaussian Scrooge ensemble, obtained as a distortion of the Haar measure over Gaussian states such that the first moment matches the Generalized Gibbs ensemble determined by the initial state of the quench. This result provides a universal description of the projected ensemble in an integrable setting with an extensive family of conservation laws directly formulated on the subsystem, extending prior results based on full-system properties~\cite{lpdd-23}. We have also identified how symmetries preserved along individual measurement trajectories restrict the accessible Gaussian-state manifold and distinguish the corresponding universal ensembles.

The physical mechanism underlying this description is the effective non-revelation of local conserved charges. Although individual measurement outcomes retain information about bath charges at finite size, this information becomes irrelevant to a finite subsystem as the bath becomes larger. This suggests that a measurement basis need not be maximally charge non-revealing at finite size to produce a Scrooge ensemble. Our analysis thus applies the charge-revelation framework of Ref.~\cite{chang-25} to integrable Gaussian dynamics, identifying conditions under which partially revealing measurements become effectively non-revealing for a large bath.

Several questions remain open. A natural direction is to extend our analysis to interacting integrable models and investigate whether effective non-revelation leads to a Scrooge ensemble determined by the reduced GGE, or whether additional signatures of integrability persist in the higher moments of the projected ensemble.

Gaussian dynamics could also provide insights into the role of conservation laws in mixed-state deep thermalization, arising from either incomplete bath measurements~\cite{yhk-25} or a mixed initial state~\cite{sr-26}.

Finally, exploring the relation between deep thermalization and measurement-induced criticality~\cite{srn-19,abd-21,poboiko-23,fava-23} offers a promising direction~\cite{ih-23,wu-26}. Building on previous studies on the role of symmetries~\cite{vasseur-22,fava-24} and their breaking~\cite{rac-26} in monitored quantum systems, our results could help clarify how these features shape the universal properties of measurement-conditioned ensembles in Gaussian fermionic systems.
